/* generated by tools/make-lessons.js from tools/lessons/svm-margin/*.js; do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = Object.assign(window.LESSONS || {}, {"svm-margin/number-line":{"idea":"Many thresholds split healthy readings from failing ones. The safest is the one farthest from the nearest reading on either side: exactly halfway across the gap. Only those two nearest readings decide where it goes.","why":"A threshold that fits the training readings but hugs one class will misjudge new readings that are only a little different. With threshold 0.4 in this page, a new failing reading at 0.3 is called healthy, though it is 0.2 from the failing group and 0.7 from the healthy group. One feature lets you see the whole idea before it turns into lines and planes.","analogy":"A teacher sets the pass mark. The best failing script has 38 marks and the weakest passing script has 58. Any mark from 39 to 58 sorts these scripts correctly, but 48 is safest: if rechecking moves a mark by up to 10, nobody flips. A pass mark of 40 flips with a 2-mark change. Scripts with 12 or 95 marks play no part in the choice.","vocab":[["Threshold","One number t. A reading above t is called failing, a reading below is called healthy. With one feature the \"boundary\" is just this point."],["Gap","The empty stretch between the highest healthy reading and the lowest failing reading. Here it runs from −0.4 to 0.5, so it is 0.9 long."],["Margin","The distance from the threshold to the nearest reading, on either side. It is half of the street: street width = 2 × margin."],["Support point","One of the two readings nearest the gap (the ringed ones). Move or delete any other reading and the best threshold stays put."],["Separable","Some threshold puts every healthy reading on one side and every failing reading on the other."],["w and b","The SVM writes its rule as <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = w·x + b</span>. With one feature w is a single number. The threshold is where f = 0, which is <span class=\"eq\">−b/w</span>."],["C","The price the SVM pays for each reading that breaks the rule (inside the street, or on the wrong side). Large C: strict. Small C: forgiving. It is a price, not a count."]],"steps":[{"h":"One feature, one cut point","p":"Each sensor reading is a single number. Healthy readings are on the left (−2.1 to −0.4) and failing ones on the right (0.5 to 2.3). A classifier is one number t: call a reading failing if it is above t. Twelve readings, one decision: where to put t."},{"h":"Many thresholds score 100%","p":"Any t between −0.4 and 0.5 gets all twelve readings right. That is a whole gap of choices, 0.9 long. Training accuracy is the same for all of them, so it cannot tell you which one to trust. (The slider starts at 0.60, which is outside the gap: the failing reading at 0.5 is on the wrong side.)"},{"h":"A threshold that hugs a class is fragile","p":"Put t at 0.4. The nearest failing reading, 0.5, is only 0.1 away. A new failing reading at 0.3 is called healthy. Put t at 0.05 instead and the same new reading is called failing. We want a number that says how much room a threshold leaves."},{"h":"Define the margin","p":"The <b>margin</b> of a threshold is the distance to the nearest reading. For a t in the gap it is <span class=\"eq\">min(t − (−0.4), 0.5 − t)</span>. If t is outside the gap it does not separate the classes, and the page draws margin 0. Plotted against t, the margin is a tent: it rises, peaks, and falls."},{"h":"The peak is the midpoint","p":"As t moves right, the distance to the healthy side grows by 1 for every 1 you move, and the distance to the failing side shrinks by 1. The smaller of two such distances is largest when they are equal: <span class=\"eq\">t* = (a + b) / 2</span>, margin <span class=\"eq\">(b − a) / 2</span>, where a = −0.4 and b = 0.5 are the two closest readings. Here t* = 0.05 and the margin is 0.45."},{"h":"The SVM says the same thing with w and b","p":"Write the rule as <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = w·x + b</span> and scale it so the two nearest readings score exactly −1 and +1. Then the street between f = −1 and f = +1 has half-width <span class=\"eq\">1/|w|</span>, which equals the margin. Making the margin large means making |w| small. This is the form that still works with 2 features, or 2000."},{"h":"One odd reading breaks the strict rule","p":"Add a failing reading at −1.2, in the middle of the healthy group. No threshold separates the classes now, so there is no largest margin. The soft margin lets readings break the rule at a price C. A large C keeps the threshold near the data (−0.10 at C = 1000). A tiny C makes the street so wide that the threshold flies off to −5.98 and every reading is called failing."}],"example":{"title":"the twelve readings, solved by hand","setup":"Healthy (label −1): <b>−2.1, −1.7, −1.4, −1.0, −0.7, −0.4</b>. Failing (label +1): <b>0.5, 0.9, 1.2, 1.6, 2.0, 2.3</b>. Find the threshold with the largest margin, then express it as w and b.","table":{"head":["Threshold t","Distance to nearest healthy","Distance to nearest failing","Margin (the smaller)","Verdict"],"rows":[["−0.20","0.20","0.70","0.20","hugs the healthy group"],["0.05","0.45","0.45","0.45","equal distances: the best"],["0.10","0.50","0.40","0.40","the midpoint of the two class averages (−1.22 and 1.42): close, but not the best"],["0.30","0.70","0.20","0.20","hugs the failing group"],["0.60","n/a","n/a","0","the failing reading at 0.5 is on the wrong side: does not separate"]]},"walk":["Find the two closest readings from opposite classes: the largest healthy one is <b>a = −0.4</b> and the smallest failing one is <b>b = 0.5</b>. For any t in the gap these are the nearest readings on each side, so the other ten never matter.","Margin at t is min(t + 0.4, 0.5 − t). The two distances are equal when t + 0.4 = 0.5 − t, so 2t = 0.1 and <b>t* = 0.05</b>. The margin is (0.5 − (−0.4)) / 2 = <b>0.45</b>.","Now the SVM form. The nearest readings must score −1 and +1: <span class=\"eq\">−0.4w + b = −1</span> and <span class=\"eq\">0.5w + b = +1</span>.","Subtract the first from the second: 0.9w = 2, so <b>w = 2.222</b> (that is 20/9). Put it back: b = 1 − 0.5 × 2.222 = <b>−0.111</b>.","Read off the threshold: <span class=\"eq\">−b/w = 0.111 / 2.222 = 0.05</span>. It matches. The half-width is 1/w = 0.45, which equals the margin, and the street runs from −0.4 to 0.5. With a hard margin on one feature, the street fills the gap exactly.","Check a reading that is not a support point: f(0.9) = 2.222 × 0.9 − 0.111 = 1.889, which is more than 1, so it lies outside the street. Move 0.9 to 2.0 and w and b do not change."],"result":"Threshold 0.05, <span class=\"eq\">w = 2.222</span>, <span class=\"eq\">b = −0.111</span>, margin 0.45, street 0.9 wide, two support points (−0.4 and 0.5). A new reading at 0.3 scores f = 0.556: positive, so failing. A new reading at 0.0 scores f = −0.111: healthy, but barely, because it is inside the street."},"read":[["Blue circles (left) and orange triangles (right)","Healthy and failing readings. Drag a reading sideways to move it."],["Heavy black vertical line, \"your threshold\"","The threshold you control. Drag it, or use the slider \"Your threshold\" (it starts at 0.60)."],["Light shaded band around your threshold","The street at your threshold: it reaches the nearest reading on each side. It is drawn only when your threshold separates the classes."],["Dashed line labelled \"SVM\", with two short dotted ticks","The threshold the SVM learned. The ticks mark the street edges, 1/|w| away on each side."],["Black rings","The support points, the readings that touch the street."],["Lower plot: blue tent","The margin of every possible threshold. It is 0 where the threshold does not separate the classes."],["Diamond labelled \"maximum 0.45\", and the black dot","The diamond is the best threshold. The dot is where your threshold stands now."],["Readout: Margin at your threshold","Distance to the nearest reading, or \"threshold does not separate\" if some reading is on the wrong side."],["Readouts: SVM threshold, half-width 1/|w|, support points","What the SVM learned: −b/w, the margin 1/|w| and how many readings touch or break the street."],["Slider: SVM penalty C","0.01 to 1000 on a log scale. At 1.0e+3 it behaves like a hard margin."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Read \"Margin at your threshold\" at the start (threshold 0.60). Then press <i>Snap to maximum margin</i>.","see":"First it says \"threshold does not separate\". After the snap the black line sits at 0.05, on top of the dashed \"SVM\" line, and both \"Margin at your threshold\" and \"Largest possible margin\" read 0.450.","why":"At 0.60 the failing reading at 0.5 is on the wrong side. The snap puts the threshold at the midpoint of the two closest opposite readings, (−0.4 + 0.5)/2, and the SVM finds the same place on its own."},{"do":"Drag the slider \"Your threshold\" from −0.4 to 0.5 slowly and watch the black dot on the lower plot.","see":"The margin readout climbs from 0 to 0.450 at 0.05, then falls back to 0 at 0.5. At −0.20 and at 0.30 it reads 0.200 both times.","why":"Inside the gap the margin is min(t + 0.4, 0.5 − t): the closer reading sets it. The two sides of the tent are mirror images around the midpoint."},{"do":"Drag a reading that is <b>not</b> ringed, for example the healthy one at −2.1, to about −1.0.","see":"Nothing important changes: \"Largest possible margin\" stays 0.450 at 0.050 and the SVM line does not move.","why":"Only the nearest healthy and nearest failing readings enter the formula (a + b)/2. A reading further away than a is never nearest to a threshold in the gap."},{"do":"Drag the ringed failing reading at 0.5 left, into the gap, to about 0.2.","see":"The tent shrinks: \"Largest possible margin\" falls to about 0.300 at about −0.100, and the SVM line follows it.","why":"The closest pair is now (−0.4, 0.2), so the gap is 0.6 and the best threshold is its midpoint, −0.1. Moving a support point moves the answer."},{"do":"Press <i>Add an odd reading</i> (a failing reading at −1.2, among the healthy ones).","see":"\"Largest possible margin\" says \"none: the groups overlap\" and the tent flattens to zero. The SVM still draws a line (threshold about −0.100, half-width 0.600, 4 support points).","why":"No threshold can separate the classes now, so a hard margin has no answer. At C = 1000 the SVM accepts one error and keeps the street narrow."},{"do":"With the odd reading in place, drag \"SVM penalty C\" from 1000 down to 0.01.","see":"The half-width grows (0.600 to 8.130) and the support points climb from 4 to 12. At C = 0.01 the SVM threshold reads −5.980, far off the left edge of the plot.","why":"A small C makes broken rules cheap, so the SVM prefers a tiny w: a huge street. With 7 failing and 6 healthy readings, the threshold slides away until every reading is called failing."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Find the <b>gap</b>, stand in the <b>middle</b>. The two readings touching the gap are the only ones that matter: the rings."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Best threshold = average of the two closest opposite readings, <span class=\"eq\">(a + b)/2</span>. Margin = half the gap between them, <span class=\"eq\">(b − a)/2</span>."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"At the best threshold the distances to the nearest reading on the left and on the right are <b>equal</b>. If they differ (0.2 and 0.7, say), slide the threshold towards the farther side until they match."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>Margin = how much a reading can wiggle before its label flips.</b> Margin 0.45 means measurement noise up to 0.45 on the nearest readings is harmless."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"<b>Margin is the half, street is the whole.</b> Street = 2 × margin = 2/|w|. On one feature with a hard margin, the street fills the gap exactly (0.9 here)."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>-svm.intercept_[0] / svm.coef_[0, 0]</code> is the threshold and <code>1 / abs(svm.coef_[0, 0])</code> is the margin. Both come from the same two numbers w and b."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The best threshold is the midpoint of the two class averages.\"","right":"It is the midpoint of the two <b>closest</b> readings. Here the class averages give 0.10 (margin 0.40), the closest pair gives 0.05 (margin 0.45). The SVM never looks at averages."},{"wrong":"\"The margin is the whole gap between the classes.\"","right":"The gap is 0.9. The margin is the distance from the threshold to the nearest reading, 0.45: half of it. The street, 2 × margin, is the whole gap."},{"wrong":"\"Adding more readings must move the best threshold.\"","right":"Only if the new reading lands <b>inside</b> or beyond the current closest pair. A new healthy reading at −3 changes nothing."},{"wrong":"\"C = 1000 means no mistakes are allowed.\"","right":"C is a price, not a ban. With the odd reading present, C = 1000 still returns a threshold, and it makes 1 training error."}],"code":[{"title":"Find the best threshold by hand: the midpoint of the closest pair","py":"import numpy as np\n\nx = np.array([-2.1, -1.7, -1.4, -1.0, -0.7, -0.4, 0.5, 0.9, 1.2, 1.6, 2.0, 2.3])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)              # -1 healthy (left), +1 failing (right)\n\na, b = x[y == -1].max(), x[y == 1].min()      # the closest healthy and closest failing reading\nprint(\"closest pair:\", a, b)\nprint(\"best threshold:\", round((a + b) / 2, 2), \" margin:\", round((b - a) / 2, 2))\nfor t in [-0.2, 0.05, 0.3]:                   # other thresholds inside the gap\n    print(\"threshold\", t, \"-> margin\", round(float(np.abs(x - t).min()), 2))","out":"closest pair: -0.4 0.5\nbest threshold: 0.05  margin: 0.45\nthreshold -0.2 -> margin 0.2\nthreshold 0.05 -> margin 0.45\nthreshold 0.3 -> margin 0.2","walk":[["y = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)","Six labels of −1 followed by six of +1. List repetition (<code>[-1] * 6</code>) is a quick way to write a label column."],["x[y == -1]","A <b>boolean mask</b>: <code>y == -1</code> is True for the healthy readings, and indexing with it keeps only those."],["x[y == -1].max(), x[y == 1].min()","The largest healthy reading and the smallest failing reading: the two edges of the gap, a and b."],["(a + b) / 2 and (b - a) / 2","The midpoint and the half-gap: the best threshold and its margin."],["np.abs(x - t).min()","The margin of any threshold t: the smallest distance from t to any reading. <code>x - t</code> subtracts t from every reading at once."]],"tip":"This only works when the classes are separable and in one feature. The SVM code below gives the same answer and also handles the odd reading."},{"title":"Let the SVM find the same threshold from w and b","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nx = np.array([-2.1, -1.7, -1.4, -1.0, -0.7, -0.4, 0.5, 0.9, 1.2, 1.6, 2.0, 2.3])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)\n\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6).fit(x.reshape(-1, 1), y)   # one feature, so one column\nw, b = svm.coef_[0, 0], svm.intercept_[0]\nprint(\"w =\", round(float(w), 3), \" b =\", round(float(b), 3))\nprint(\"threshold -b/w =\", round(float(-b / w), 3), \" half-width 1/w =\", round(float(1 / w), 3))\nprint(\"support readings:\", svm.support_vectors_.ravel().tolist())","out":"w = 2.222  b = -0.111\nthreshold -b/w = 0.05  half-width 1/w = 0.45\nsupport readings: [-0.4, 0.5]","walk":[["x.reshape(-1, 1)","sklearn wants a table: rows are readings, columns are features. <code>reshape(-1, 1)</code> turns the 12 numbers into 12 rows and 1 column. The <code>-1</code> means \"work out the row count yourself\"."],["SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6)","A linear SVM with a huge price for breaking the rule, so on separable data it behaves like a hard margin."],["svm.coef_[0, 0]","The learned w. <code>coef_</code> is a 2-D array (one row per boundary, one column per feature), so a single feature needs <code>[0, 0]</code>."],["-b / w and 1 / w","The threshold is where f(x) = w·x + b equals 0, so x = −b/w. The street edges are at f = ±1, so the half-width is 1/w."],["svm.support_vectors_.ravel()","The readings that touch the street, as a column; <code>ravel()</code> flattens it to a plain row."]],"tip":"The numbers w = 2.222 and b = −0.111 are what you get by solving the two edge equations by hand."},{"title":"Add an odd reading and sweep C","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nx = np.array([-2.1, -1.7, -1.4, -1.0, -0.7, -0.4, 0.5, 0.9, 1.2, 1.6, 2.0, 2.3, -1.2])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6 + [1])       # the last reading, at -1.2, is an odd \"failing\" one\nX = x.reshape(-1, 1)\n\nfor C in [1000, 1, 0.1, 0.01]:\n    svm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=C).fit(X, y)\n    w, b = svm.coef_[0, 0], svm.intercept_[0]\n    errors = int((svm.predict(X) != y).sum())\n    print(f\"C={C:<6} threshold={-b / w:6.2f}  half-width={1 / w:5.2f}  support={len(svm.support_):2d}  errors={errors}\")","out":"C=1000   threshold= -0.10  half-width= 0.60  support= 4  errors=1\nC=1      threshold= -0.05  half-width= 0.95  support= 6  errors=1\nC=0.1    threshold= -0.05  half-width= 1.65  support=10  errors=1\nC=0.01   threshold= -5.98  half-width= 8.13  support=12  errors=6","walk":[["[1] ... + [1]","The last label is +1 even though the reading sits among the healthy ones: that is what makes it \"odd\"."],["for C in [1000, 1, 0.1, 0.01]","Refit the same model at four prices. C is a value you choose before fitting, not something the model learns."],["svm.support_","The row numbers of the support points. <code>len(...)</code> counts them: 4 at the strict end, 12 of the 13 readings at the lenient end."],["svm.predict(X) != y","A True/False list marking the readings the model gets wrong; <code>.sum()</code> counts the Trues."],["f\"C={C:<6} ... {x:6.2f}\"","An f-string. <code>:&lt;6</code> pads on the right to width 6, <code>:6.2f</code> means width 6 with 2 decimals, so the columns line up."]],"tip":"At C = 0.01 the 6 errors are exactly the six healthy readings: the threshold has left the data and everything is called failing."}],"check":[{"q":"Why is the threshold halfway between the two closest opposite readings the best one?","options":["The distances to the nearest reading on each side are equal, so the smaller of the two is as large as it can be","It is the average of all the readings","It is the only threshold with zero training errors","It makes the street as narrow as possible"],"answer":0,"why":"Many thresholds have zero training errors, so that cannot be the reason. The margin is the smaller of the two distances, and it is largest when they are equal, which is the midpoint."},{"q":"A trained SVM on one feature has <code>coef_ = [[0.25]]</code> and <code>intercept_ = [-4.0]</code>. What are the threshold and the margin?","options":["threshold 4, margin 16","threshold −4, margin 0.25","threshold 16, margin 8","threshold 16, margin 4"],"answer":3,"why":"The threshold is −b/w = 4 / 0.25 = 16. The margin (half-width) is 1/w = 4. The street, 2/w = 8, is twice the margin."},{"q":"You drag the closest failing reading from 0.5 to 0.2, towards the healthy group (closest healthy reading: −0.4). What happens to the best threshold and the margin?","options":["The threshold stays at 0.05 and the margin falls to 0.3","The threshold moves to −0.1 and the margin stays 0.45","The threshold moves to −0.1 and the margin falls to 0.3","Nothing changes, because this reading is not a support point"],"answer":2,"why":"This reading is a support point, so moving it changes the answer. The new closest pair is (−0.4, 0.2): midpoint −0.1, margin (0.2 + 0.4)/2 = 0.3."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend where to put the threshold between two groups on a number line and which readings decide it.","model":"Many thresholds sort the training readings correctly, so I pick the one with the most room: halfway between the highest healthy and the lowest failing reading, where the distance to the nearest reading is the same on both sides. Only those two closest readings matter, and the distance to them is the margin."},"cheat":"Margin of a threshold = distance to the nearest reading. Best threshold = midpoint of the two closest opposite readings: t = (a + b)/2, margin (b − a)/2. The SVM writes the same thing as f(x) = w·x + b with threshold −b/w, margin 1/|w|, street 2/|w|. One odd reading removes the hard margin; C prices the broken rule."},"svm-margin/max-margin":{"idea":"Many lines can split two groups of points. A support vector machine picks the one with the <b>widest empty street</b> around it, and only the few points touching that street decide where it goes.","why":"A line that squeezes past one group will misjudge new customers who are only slightly different from the ones you trained on. The line with the most room on both sides is the safest guess for points you have not seen yet.","analogy":"Painting the centre line on a road between two rows of parked cars. You could paint it hugging one row, but the careful choice is the middle, with the most space on both sides. Only the car nearest the line on each side decides where the middle is. Cars further back do not matter.","vocab":[["Decision boundary","The line (in 2 features) or flat surface (in more) where the model switches from one class to the other: all points with <span class=\"eq\">w·x + b = 0</span>."],["Margin","The distance from the boundary to the nearest point, <span class=\"eq\">1/‖w‖</span>. It is half of the street."],["Street","The empty band around the boundary, <span class=\"eq\">2/‖w‖</span> wide: twice the margin. Its two edges are <span class=\"eq\">w·x + b = +1</span> and <span class=\"eq\">−1</span>."],["Support vector","A training point sitting exactly on an edge of the street. Remove it and the boundary moves. Remove any other point and nothing changes."],["w and b","<b>w</b> is the weight vector. It points across the street and sets its direction and width. <b>b</b> slides the line sideways."],["Hard margin","The strict rule: no point may be inside the street or on the wrong side. It only exists when the groups are <i>separable</i>."],["Separable","At least one straight line puts all of class A on one side and all of class B on the other."]],"steps":[{"h":"Start with the plain problem","p":"Each customer is a point, described by two numbers. Blue means repaid, orange means defaulted. A classifier is a rule that colours the plane: everything on one side of a line is predicted blue, the rest orange."},{"h":"Many lines do the job","p":"If the groups do not touch, there are infinitely many lines that put every training point on its correct side. The faint grey lines in the simulation are a few of them. All score 100% on the training data, so <b>training accuracy cannot choose between them</b>."},{"h":"A good line leaves room","p":"Tilt a line until it almost touches one blue point. It still classifies the training data correctly, but a new blue customer a little to the right would land on the orange side. A line far from both groups tolerates more variation in new data."},{"h":"Measure the room: the margin","p":"For a line <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = w·x + b = 0</span>, the distance from a point to the line is <span class=\"eq\">|f(x)| / ‖w‖</span>. Scale w and b so that the nearest points on each side have <span class=\"eq\">f = +1</span> and <span class=\"eq\">f = −1</span>. Then the nearest points are <span class=\"eq\">1/‖w‖</span> from the line (the <b>margin</b>) and the street between them is <b>2 / ‖w‖</b> wide."},{"h":"Widest street means smallest ‖w‖","p":"Making 2/‖w‖ big is the same as making ‖w‖ small. So the SVM minimises <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖²</span> (squaring just makes the maths easier) subject to <span class=\"eq\">yᵢ(w·xᵢ + b) ≥ 1</span> for every point. With yᵢ = ±1, that one inequality says: correct side <i>and</i> outside the street."},{"h":"Only the touching points matter","p":"At the answer, the inequality is an exact equality (yᵢ f(xᵢ) = 1) only for points on the street edges: the support vectors. All other points satisfy it with room to spare, so moving or deleting them cannot change the best line. The page shows this as \"Delete a non-support point: change in line\" reading max 0.0e+0."},{"h":"When the groups overlap, hard margin fails","p":"If no line separates the groups, the inequalities cannot all hold and there is no answer. One stray point can also squeeze the street very narrow. The <b>soft margin</b> (next simulation) fixes both by letting points break the rule at a price, called C."}],"example":{"title":"three customers, solved by hand","setup":"Two features per customer (think: a standardised income score and a savings score). One defaulted, at <b>(1, 1)</b>, label −1. Two repaid, at <b>(3, 3)</b> and <b>(4, 3)</b>, label +1.","table":{"head":["Point","Label y","f(x) = 0.5·x₁ + 0.5·x₂ − 2","y · f(x)","Role"],"rows":[["(1, 1)","−1","−1.0","1.0","on the street edge: support vector"],["(3, 3)","+1","+1.0","1.0","on the street edge: support vector"],["(4, 3)","+1","+1.5","1.5","outside the street: not a support vector"]]},"walk":["Guess: the closest opposite pair is (1,1) and (3,3). The best line should sit halfway between them, passing through the midpoint (2, 2), at right angles to the segment joining them.","That segment points along (1, 1), so <b>w</b> must point that way too: w = (a, a).","Edge conditions: <span class=\"eq\">w·(3,3) + b = +1</span> gives 6a + b = 1, and <span class=\"eq\">w·(1,1) + b = −1</span> gives 2a + b = −1.","Subtract the second from the first: 4a = 2, so <b>a = 0.5</b>, and then <b>b = −2</b>. So w = (0.5, 0.5), b = −2.","Street width = 2/‖w‖ = 2 / √(0.25 + 0.25) = 2 / 0.707 = <b>2.83</b>. That equals the distance between (1,1) and (3,3), which is √8 = 2.83. The two answers agree.","Check the third point: f(4, 3) = 0.5·4 + 0.5·3 − 2 = 1.5, which is more than 1. It is safely outside the street, so it is <b>not</b> a support vector. Delete it, or move it to (9, 3), and w and b do not change."],"result":"Two support vectors, <span class=\"eq\">w = (0.5, 0.5)</span>, <span class=\"eq\">b = −2</span>, street width 2.83 (margin 1.41). A new customer at (2.2, 2.2) gets f = 0.2: positive, so \"repaid\", but only just, because it sits close to the boundary. The size of f tells you the distance, and its sign tells you the side."},"read":[["Blue circles and orange triangles","The two classes. Click the plot to add points; drag a point to move it."],["Solid black line","The maximum-margin boundary, where <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = 0</span>."],["Dashed lines","The street edges, where <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = ±1</span>. The street is the empty band between them."],["Black rings around points","The support vectors: the points touching a dashed edge."],["Faint grey lines","Other lines that also separate the data (tick box \"Show other separating lines\"). They are the \"many lines do the job\" from step 2, and they are re-drawn at random after each edit."],["Readout: Separable?","\"yes\" if some line splits the classes. \"no: the groups overlap\" means a hard margin cannot exist, and the other readouts show \"–\"."],["Readouts: w and b","The learned weights and offset. A bigger ‖w‖ means a narrower street."],["Readout: Street width","The value of 2/‖w‖, in the same units as the axes."],["Readout: Support vectors","How many points touch the street, written like \"3 of 28\"."],["Readout: Delete a non-support point: change in line","The largest movement of the boundary over every way of deleting one non-support point, written like \"max 0.0e+0\". Zero means none of them moves it."],["Readout: Delete a support vector: change in line","The median movement when you delete one support vector, written like \"median 0.183\". It is clearly above zero."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Keep <i>Start from</i> on <i>Two separated groups</i>. Under \"Click on the plot to…\" choose <i>remove</i> and click two or three blue circles that are <b>not</b> ringed.","see":"The black line and dashed lines do not move: street width stays 1.164 and w stays about (1.62, 0.57). \"Delete a non-support point: change in line\" keeps reading max 0.0e+0. (The faint grey lines are re-drawn at random: ignore them.)","why":"A point strictly outside the street satisfies yᵢ f(xᵢ) > 1 with room to spare. The best line was decided by the points on the edges, so removing a far point cannot change it."},{"do":"On <i>Two separated groups</i>, drag one ringed point about 0.3 units away from the street, towards its own group.","see":"The street gets wider, from 1.164 to about 1.18 to 1.46 depending on which ringed point you move, and the ring may jump to another point.","why":"The street can only grow until it touches a point again. The nearest points always define the width, so moving a support vector changes the street, while moving a far point does not."},{"do":"Switch <i>Start from</i> to <i>Separated groups + one odd point</i>.","see":"Street width reads 0.467 and w grows to (4.04, 1.43). Without the odd orange point the same groups would have a street of 1.716. The line itself turns by only about 1.4°.","why":"A hard margin obeys every point without exception, so a single outlier can dictate the whole boundary. This weakness is exactly why the soft margin exists."},{"do":"Switch <i>Start from</i> to <i>Overlapping groups</i>.","see":"Readout \"Separable?\" says \"no: the groups overlap\", and the street, the other lines and the other readouts disappear.","why":"When no line can put every point on its correct side, the constraints cannot all be met, so a hard-margin SVM has no solution."},{"do":"On <i>Two separated groups</i>, use <i>add blue</i> and <i>add orange</i> under \"Click on the plot to…\" to place four or five new points of each colour near the far edges, blue on the left and orange on the right.","see":"The line does not move: w stays (1.62, 0.57), the street stays 1.164 and \"Support vectors\" still shows 3.","why":"Far points are not support vectors. Only points touching the street have any influence, which is why SVMs are said to depend on a small subset of the training data."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Widest <b>street</b>, touching <b>cars</b> decide. The rings are the cars that touch the line."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>Small w, wide street.</b> Width is 2/‖w‖, so shrinking ‖w‖ widens the street. \"Minimise ‖w‖\" and \"maximise the margin\" are the same instruction."},{"tag":"Read the sign and size","text":"For any point, <b>sign of f(x)</b> is the predicted side and <b>|f(x)|</b> is how far it is from the line (in units of 1/‖w‖). |f| = 1 means exactly on a street edge."},{"tag":"Decode the inequality","text":"<span class=\"eq\">y·f(x) ≥ 1</span> reads as \"on the correct side <i>and</i> outside the street\" in one go. If y·f is between 0 and 1 the point is correct but inside the street; below 0 it is wrong."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Few support vectors usually means a clean, well-separated problem. If almost every point is a support vector, the classes overlap a lot."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The SVM line is the line closest to the points.\"","right":"It is the line <b>farthest</b> from the nearest points on both sides: it maximises the smallest distance."},{"wrong":"\"Every training point affects the boundary.\"","right":"Only the <b>support vectors</b> do. Delete the rest and the boundary stays exactly where it was."},{"wrong":"\"The street width is ‖w‖.\"","right":"It is <b>2/‖w‖</b>. They move in opposite directions: big ‖w‖, thin street."},{"wrong":"\"A hard-margin SVM works on any data.\"","right":"Only on <b>separable</b> data. With overlap or a stray point you need the soft margin (parameter C)."}],"code":[{"title":"Fit a (nearly) hard-margin linear SVM and read what it learned","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX = np.array([[1, 1], [3, 3], [4, 3]])   # three customers, two features\ny = np.array([-1, 1, 1])                  # -1 = defaulted, +1 = repaid\n\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6)         # huge C: (almost) no violations allowed\nsvm.fit(X, y)\n\nprint(\"w =\", np.round(svm.coef_[0], 3), \" b =\", round(float(svm.intercept_[0]), 3))\nprint(\"support vectors:\", svm.support_vectors_.tolist())\nprint(\"street width:\", round(2 / float(np.linalg.norm(svm.coef_[0])), 3))","out":"w = [0.5 0.5]  b = -2.0\nsupport vectors: [[1.0, 1.0], [3.0, 3.0]]\nstreet width: 2.828","walk":[["SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6)","Build the model object. <code>kernel=\"linear\"</code> means a straight boundary. <code>C</code> is the price of breaking the rules; a huge C makes it behave like a hard margin."],["svm.fit(X, y)","<code>fit</code> learns from the data. Features first (a 2-D array, one row per customer), labels second."],["svm.coef_[0]","The learned <b>w</b>. Attributes that end in an underscore are results of fitting. <code>coef_</code> has one row per boundary, hence <code>[0]</code>."],["svm.intercept_[0]","The learned <b>b</b>."],["svm.support_vectors_","The training rows that ended up on the street edges: exactly the rings in the picture."],["2 / np.linalg.norm(w)","The street width formula 2/‖w‖. <code>np.linalg.norm</code> computes ‖w‖."]],"tip":"Labels may be any two values (−1/+1, 0/1, strings). Only the features must be numbers."},{"title":"Score a new customer: side and confidence","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX = np.array([[1, 1], [3, 3], [4, 3]])\ny = np.array([-1, 1, 1])\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6).fit(X, y)    # fit returns the model, so you can chain\n\nnew = np.array([[2.2, 2.2]])                   # note the double brackets: one row\nprint(\"f(x) =\", np.round(svm.decision_function(new), 3))\nprint(\"class:\", svm.predict(new))","out":"f(x) = [0.2]\nclass: [1]","walk":[[".fit(X, y)","Returns the fitted model itself, so <code>SVC(...).fit(X, y)</code> builds and trains in one line."],["new = np.array([[2.2, 2.2]])","sklearn always wants a 2-D table: rows are customers, columns are features. One customer is a table with one row, hence the double brackets."],["svm.decision_function(new)","Returns f(x) = w·x + b: the signed distance score. Here 0.5·2.2 + 0.5·2.2 − 2 = 0.2."],["svm.predict(new)","Just the sign of f turned into a class label: positive gives +1."]],"tip":"If the numbers disagree with your hand calculation, check that you used the same scaling. SVMs need scaled features (see the scaling simulation)."}],"check":[{"q":"A trained SVM has <span class=\"eq\">w = (2, 0)</span>. How wide is the street?","options":["1","2","0.5","4"],"answer":0,"why":"Width = 2/‖w‖ = 2/2 = 1. A bigger ‖w‖ means a narrower street."},{"q":"You delete a training point that is <b>not</b> a support vector and refit a hard-margin SVM. What happens to the boundary?","options":["The street gets wider","It moves slightly","It does not change","The SVM becomes soft-margin"],"answer":2,"why":"Only support vectors define the boundary. A point with yᵢ f(xᵢ) > 1 has no say, so the same line is still optimal."},{"q":"Which condition means \"point i is on its correct side and outside the street\"?","options":["yᵢ = sign(b)","‖w‖ ≥ 1","w·xᵢ + b = 0","yᵢ (w·xᵢ + b) ≥ 1"],"answer":3,"why":"y is ±1 and f(x) has the sign of the side, so y·f ≥ 1 means correct side with at least 1 unit to spare, which is exactly outside the street."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why an SVM chooses the widest street, and which points control the result.","model":"Lots of lines split the training data, so the SVM picks the one with the most empty space around it, because that leaves the most room for new points to still land on the right side. Only the points that touch the edge of the street (the support vectors) set the line; deleting any other point changes nothing."},"cheat":"SVM = widest street between the classes. Street width = 2/‖w‖, so minimise ‖w‖ subject to y(w·x + b) ≥ 1. Only support vectors (on the street edges) decide the line. Hard margin needs separable data; overlap needs the soft margin (C)."},"svm-margin/soft-margin":{"idea":"The soft margin lets points sit inside the street, or even on the wrong side, at a price of C per unit of violation. Small C: wide street, mistakes tolerated. Large C: narrow street that bends to individual points.","why":"Real classes overlap, and one odd customer can make a hard margin impossible or squeeze it to almost nothing: in the odd-point preset the street shrinks from 1.602 to 0.286 as C rises. C is the dial between trusting every training point and trusting the overall shape. Set it badly and the model either ignores structure or memorises noise.","analogy":"A school wants an empty buffer strip between two playgrounds, as wide as possible. Footballs keep landing in it. Each ball inside the strip, or on the wrong playground, is fined C rupees per metre of intrusion. At ₹0.01 the school keeps a huge strip and pays for many intrusions. At ₹1000 it squeezes the strip until almost no ball lands inside, even if that means a thin strip tilted to dodge one stray ball.","vocab":[["Slack ξ (xi)","How far a point falls short of the street rule: <span class=\"eq\">ξ = max(0, 1 − y·f(x))</span>. It is 0 for a point on or outside its street edge."],["C","The price of one unit of slack. Not a count of allowed mistakes. The cost to minimise is <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C·Σξ</span>."],["Soft margin","The SVM that allows slack, so it always has an answer, even when the classes overlap. The hard margin is the limit of very large C."],["Support vector (soft margin)","Any point with <span class=\"eq\">y·f ≤ 1</span>: on a street edge, inside the street, or on the wrong side. Only points outside the street are not support vectors."],["Training error","A training point on the wrong side of the line, <span class=\"eq\">y·f &lt; 0</span>. It has slack above 1."],["Cross-validated accuracy","Split the data into 5 folds. Train on 4, test on the 5th, rotate, and average. It estimates accuracy on points the model did not see."],["Plateau","Past some C, nothing changes any more. On overlapping data, raising C further cannot buy fewer violations."]],"steps":[{"h":"The hard margin cracks","p":"The hard margin needs every point outside the street on its own side. With overlapping classes no line can do that, so there is no answer. With one odd point, the answer exists but can be terrible: in the \"Separated groups + one odd point\" preset the street is 0.286 wide at C = 1000, against 1.602 at C = 1."},{"h":"Let a point break the rule, and measure it","p":"Give each point a slack ξ ≥ 0 and relax its rule to <span class=\"eq\">y·f(x) ≥ 1 − ξ</span>. If ξ = 0 the point obeys. If 0 &lt; ξ &lt; 1 it is inside the street but still on its side. If ξ ≥ 1 it is on the boundary or the wrong side: a training error. Every dataset now has an answer: pick big enough slacks."},{"h":"Charge for slack","p":"Two wishes now fight: a wide street (small ‖w‖) and little slack. Combine them: minimise <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C·Σξᵢ</span>. A unit of ½‖w‖² costs 1 and a unit of slack costs C. So C is an exchange rate. Cheap slack (small C) gives in to the wish for width. Expensive slack (large C) gives in to the points."},{"h":"Slack is the hinge loss","p":"For fixed w and b, the cheapest slack that satisfies both ξ ≥ 0 and ξ ≥ 1 − y·f is <span class=\"eq\">ξ = max(0, 1 − y·f)</span>. That is the <b>hinge loss</b>: zero beyond the street, rising by 1 for every unit you go inside. So the soft margin is \"½‖w‖² plus C times total hinge loss\". The next simulation compares it with log-loss."},{"h":"What the extremes look like","p":"As C shrinks, slack is free, w shrinks, the street balloons and every point ends up a support vector (48 of 48 at C = 0.01 in the overlapping preset). As C grows on separable data, the answer approaches the hard margin. On overlapping data it stops changing at about C = 15: some violations cannot be avoided."},{"h":"Who is a support vector now","p":"In <span class=\"eq\">w = Σ αᵢ yᵢ xᵢ</span> each point has a weight α between 0 and C. Points outside the street have α = 0. Points exactly on an edge have 0 &lt; α &lt; C. Points inside the street or on the wrong side sit at the cap α = C. A point cannot pull harder than C, however far wrong it is."},{"h":"Choose C by cross-validation","p":"Training accuracy is a poor guide: it is not what the SVM minimises, so it can even fall as C grows. Try C on a log grid (0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100), score each with cross-validation, and take a C from the broad best region. It is the bias-variance dial: small C is stable but stiff, large C is flexible but jumpy."}],"example":{"title":"thirteen applicants: two lines, two prices","setup":"Card utilisation (%). Repaid, label −1: <b>12, 18, 22, 27, 33, 38, 55</b> (the one at 55 is odd). Defaulted, label +1: <b>58, 64, 70, 77, 83, 90</b>. Compare two lines. <b>Soft line:</b> threshold 51, <span class=\"eq\">w = 1/13</span>, so <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = (x − 51)/13</span>. <b>Hard line:</b> threshold 56.5, <span class=\"eq\">w = 1/1.5</span>.","table":{"head":["Applicant","y","f(x) on the soft line","y · f","Slack ξ = max(0, 1 − y·f)","Where it is"],"rows":[["38","−1","−1.0000","1.0000","0","on its street edge"],["55","−1","0.3077","−0.3077","1.3077","wrong side: ξ > 1"],["58","+1","0.5385","0.5385","0.4615","inside the street, right side"],["64","+1","1.0000","1.0000","0","on its street edge"],["other 9","","beyond ±1","> 1","0","outside the street"]]},"walk":["Score every applicant on the soft line, <span class=\"eq\">f = (x − 51)/13</span>. For 55 (repaid, so y = −1): f = 4/13 = 0.3077, so y·f = −0.3077. Slack = 1 − (−0.3077) = <b>1.3077</b>. It is on the wrong side.","For 58 (defaulted, y = +1): f = 7/13 = 0.5385, y·f = 0.5385, slack = 1 − 0.5385 = <b>0.4615</b>. Inside the street, but on the right side. The other nine have y·f ≥ 1, so zero slack. Total <b>Σξ = 1.7692</b>.","Cost of the soft line at C = 0.01: <span class=\"eq\">½(1/13)² + 0.01 × 1.7692 = 0.00296 + 0.01769 = <b>0.02065</b></span>.","Cost of the hard line: every applicant is outside its street (55 and 58 sit exactly on the edges), so Σξ = 0 and the cost is just <span class=\"eq\">½(1/1.5)² = <b>0.2222</b></span>, for every C.","At C = 0.01 the soft line costs 0.02065 against 0.2222, ten times less, so it wins. At C = 1 the soft line costs 0.00296 + 1.7692 = 1.7722, the hard line still 0.2222, so the hard line wins. These two lines tie at <span class=\"eq\">C = (0.2222 − 0.00296)/1.7692 = 0.124</span>.","The prediction flips with the winner. Applicant 52: soft line f = 1/13 = 0.077, positive, so <b>defaulted</b> (barely). Hard line f = (52 − 56.5)/1.5 = −3, so <b>repaid</b>."],"result":"The same data and the same two lines, but C decides the winner. SVC at C = 0.01 returns exactly the soft line (threshold 51, 4 support vectors: 38, 55, 58, 64) and at C = 1 exactly the hard line. At C = 0.1 a third compromise line (threshold 56.5, half-width 3.33, cost 0.155) is cheaper than both. C is a price, not a count of mistakes."},"read":[["Blue circles and orange triangles","Class A and class B. Use \"Click on the plot to…\" to add blue, add orange or remove a point. Dragging a point always moves it."],["Tinted regions","Predicted class at each place: blue side and orange side. The tint deepens with the distance from the line."],["Solid black line, two dashed lines","The boundary <span class=\"eq\">f = 0</span> and the street edges <span class=\"eq\">f = ±1</span>, the orange dashes on the orange side and the blue dashes on the blue side."],["Black rings","Support vectors. Unlike the hard-margin page, rings now include points inside the street and on the wrong side."],["Short black segments","The slack of a rule-breaking point: a segment from the point to its own class's dashed edge. Its length is ξ/‖w‖. A point on the wrong side gets a segment that crosses the whole street."],["Readouts: Street width, Support vectors, Training errors, Training accuracy","Street width is 2/‖w‖. \"Support vectors\" reads like \"21 of 48\". Errors are points on the wrong side."],["Readout: 5-fold cross-validated accuracy","Stratified 5-fold with a fixed shuffle, so the same data and C always give the same number."],["Readouts: ½‖w‖² and C · Σξ","The two parts of the cost. Divide C · Σξ by C to get Σξ, the raw violation total."],["Left plot: dashed and solid curves","Dashed is training accuracy, solid is cross-validated accuracy, against C on a log scale. The vertical dashed line is your C. The diamond marks the best cross-validated accuracy."],["Right plot: street width against C","The width at every C. It falls as C rises and then goes flat."]],"experiments":[{"do":"On <i>Overlapping groups</i>, drag C from the far left (0.01) to the far right (1.0e+3) and compare the readouts at the two ends.","see":"Street width falls from 5.462 to 1.218. Support vectors fall from \"48 of 48\" to \"18 of 48\". ½‖w‖² grows from 0.07 to 1.35. Cross-validated accuracy moves only from 81.3% to 83.3%.","why":"At small C slack is almost free, so the SVM prefers a tiny w, a huge street, and every point inside or on it. At large C slack is dear, so the street must shrink to keep most points outside it."},{"do":"Drag C from about 20 up to the far right and watch the readouts.","see":"Street width stays 1.218 and support vectors stay \"18 of 48\". Only C · Σξ keeps growing, from about 350 to about 17,690, because C multiplies it.","why":"This is the plateau. The classes overlap, so some violations stay however much you charge. Σξ is stuck near 17.69, and the same line is optimal at every larger C."},{"do":"On <i>Overlapping groups</i>, read Training accuracy with C at 1 and then with C at the far right.","see":"It is 88% (6 errors) at C = 1 and 83% (8 errors) at C = 1.0e+3. It went down while C went up.","why":"The SVM does not minimise the number of errors. It minimises hinge loss, which also counts how far inside the street the correct points are. A line with the lower hinge total can have more errors."},{"do":"Choose <i>Separated groups + one odd point</i> (before pressing New sample). Compare C about 1 with C at the far right.","see":"At C ≈ 1: width 1.602, \"4 of 27\" support vectors, 1 training error, cross-validated 96.3%. At the far right: width 0.286, \"3 of 27\", 0 errors, but cross-validated 92.6%. The boundary has twisted to put the odd orange point on its own side.","why":"At large C the odd point is too expensive to ignore, so the line bends round it and the street shrinks. Training accuracy reaches 100%, but the line is now fitted to one unusual point, which costs accuracy on unseen points."},{"do":"Choose <i>Two separated groups</i>, press <i>Add an outlier</i> (a blue point inside the orange group), and compare C about 1 with C at the far right.","see":"The new point is on the wrong side at every C. At C ≈ 1 there is 1 training error and a street of 1.555. At the far right there are 2 errors and a street of 1.312.","why":"A point deep in the other class can never be fitted by a straight line. Its pull is capped at C, so it cannot drag the line far, but the line tilts and gives up one more point."},{"do":"On <i>Overlapping groups</i>, press <i>New sample</i> several times and read the diamond label \"best CV at C ≈\".","see":"The label jumps around: about 0.0316, 0.178, 0.316, 0.0562, 0.0562 over the first five presses. It is never at the far right. The gain over C = 1.0e+3 is between 0 and 4 points.","why":"With 48 points one point is 2.1% accuracy, so the exact peak is noise. Trust the broad region (small to middle C), not the tip of the curve."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Fines for trespass.</b> C is the fine per metre. Cheap fines: wide strip, many trespassers (many support vectors). Dear fines: thin strip, few trespassers."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Cost = (a wish for a wide street) + C × (total rule-breaking). <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C·Σξ</span>, with <span class=\"eq\">ξ = max(0, 1 − y·f)</span>."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Compute <span class=\"eq\">y·f</span> first. Above 1: slack 0. Between 0 and 1: slack is 1 − y·f, inside the street. Below 0: wrong side, slack above 1. A negative y·f (not a negative f) is the error sign."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Search C on a <b>log grid</b> (×10 or ×3 steps), because C acts as a multiplier. If training accuracy is far above cross-validated accuracy, lower C."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"<b>Small C means small w.</b> At C = 0.01 the street is 5.46 wide, wider than the whole cloud of points (4.6 across), so no point reaches an edge. A street that wide is a warning that C is too low."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>SVC(C=1.0)</code> is the default. <code>len(svm.support_)</code> counts support vectors. <code>GridSearchCV(SVC(kernel=\"linear\"), {\"C\": [...]})</code> picks C by cross-validation."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"C is the number of mistakes the SVM may make.\"","right":"C is a <b>price per unit of slack</b>. At C = 0.01 the overlapping preset still makes 9 training errors, and at C = 1000 it makes 8: nearly the same count, a very different street."},{"wrong":"\"A bigger C always gives higher training accuracy.\"","right":"Not even that. In the overlapping preset it is 88% at C = 1 and 83% at C = 1000. The SVM minimises hinge loss, not the error count."},{"wrong":"\"Support vectors are the misclassified points.\"","right":"They are every point with <span class=\"eq\">y·f ≤ 1</span>: misclassified, inside the street, and exactly on an edge. At C = 0.01 all 48 points are support vectors."},{"wrong":"\"The soft margin is only for overlapping data.\"","right":"On separable data too. In the <i>Two separated groups</i> preset a small C gives a wide street with every point a support vector (28 of 28 at C = 0.01) and a larger C gives the 0.640-wide hard margin."}],"code":[{"title":"Compute slack and the cost by hand on thirteen applicants","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nx = np.array([12, 18, 22, 27, 33, 38, 55, 58, 64, 70, 77, 83, 90])   # card utilisation, %\ny = np.array([-1] * 7 + [1] * 6)       # -1 repaid (55 is the odd one), +1 defaulted\nC, X = 0.01, x.reshape(-1, 1)\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=C).fit(X, y)\nw = svm.coef_[0, 0]\nslack = np.maximum(0, 1 - y * svm.decision_function(X))   # 0 if outside the street\nprint(\"threshold\", round(float(-svm.intercept_[0] / w), 2), \" half-width\", round(float(1 / w), 2))\nprint(\"slack:\", {int(a): round(float(s), 4) for a, s in zip(x, slack) if s > 0.001})\nprint(\"objective:\", round(float(0.5 * w**2 + C * slack.sum()), 5))","out":"threshold 51.0  half-width 13.0\nslack: {55: 1.3077, 58: 0.4615}\nobjective: 0.02065","walk":[["y * svm.decision_function(X)","The score <span class=\"eq\">f = w·x + b</span> of every applicant, times the label, gives <span class=\"eq\">y·f</span>. Positive means correct side; at least 1 means outside the street."],["np.maximum(0, 1 - y * f)","Slack: <code>np.maximum</code> compares element by element, so negative values (points safely outside) become 0. This is the hinge loss."],["zip(x, slack)","Pairs each applicant with its slack so the dictionary comprehension can keep only the rule breakers (<code>s &gt; 0.001</code> ignores rounding dust)."],["0.5 * w**2 + C * slack.sum()","The soft-margin cost: ½w² (the wide-street wish) plus C times the total slack. <code>w**2</code> is \"w squared\"."],["float(...)","Turns a one-element NumPy value into an ordinary Python number so <code>round</code> prints cleanly."]],"tip":"The printed objective 0.02065 is the number you computed by hand in the worked example."},{"title":"Sweep C: width, support vectors, training and cross-validated accuracy","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\nimport numpy as np\n\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=80, n_features=30, n_informative=4, n_redundant=0, random_state=3)\nfor C in [0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 100]:\n    svm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=C).fit(X, y)\n    width = 2 / np.linalg.norm(svm.coef_[0])          # street width 2/||w||\n    cv = cross_val_score(SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=C), X, y, cv=5).mean()\n    print(f\"C={C:<6} width={width:6.2f} support={len(svm.support_):2d} train={svm.score(X, y):.3f} cv={cv:.3f}\")","out":"C=0.001  width= 22.28 support=80 train=0.863 cv=0.738\nC=0.01   width=  3.53 support=71 train=0.863 cv=0.812\nC=0.1    width=  1.53 support=46 train=0.963 cv=0.775\nC=1      width=  0.64 support=27 train=0.988 cv=0.787\nC=100    width=  0.49 support=26 train=1.000 cv=0.762","walk":[["make_classification(..., random_state=3)","Builds a made-up dataset: 80 rows, 30 features of which only 4 carry signal. Many noisy features are what makes a large C overfit. <code>random_state</code> fixes the draw."],["np.linalg.norm(svm.coef_[0])","‖w‖, the length of the weight vector. The street width is 2 divided by it."],["cross_val_score(SVC(...), X, y, cv=5).mean()","Five fits, each tested on the part it did not see. It builds a <i>new</i> unfitted model each time, so the earlier <code>fit</code> is not reused. The mean is the cross-validated accuracy."],["svm.score(X, y)","Accuracy on the same rows it trained on: training accuracy. It can only flatter the model."],["f\"{width:6.2f}\"","An f-string format: 6 characters wide, 2 decimals, so the columns line up."]],"tip":"Read down the columns: width and support vectors fall steadily, training accuracy climbs to 1.000, but the cross-validated accuracy is highest at C = 0.01 and never beats that again."},{"title":"Let GridSearchCV pick C","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=80, n_features=30, n_informative=4, n_redundant=0, random_state=3)\ngrid = {\"C\": [0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 100]}           # log-spaced: each step is x10\nsearch = GridSearchCV(SVC(kernel=\"linear\"), grid, cv=5).fit(X, y)\nprint(\"best:\", search.best_params_, round(search.best_score_, 3))\nprint(\"model refit on all data:\", search.best_estimator_.C)","out":"best: {'C': 0.01} 0.812\nmodel refit on all data: 0.01","walk":[["grid = {\"C\": [...]}","A dictionary: the key is the name of the parameter to vary and the value is the list of settings to try."],["GridSearchCV(SVC(kernel=\"linear\"), grid, cv=5)","Tries every setting with 5-fold cross-validation and records the mean score of each."],[".fit(X, y)","Runs the search, then refits the best setting on <i>all</i> of the data."],["search.best_params_ and best_score_","The winning C and its cross-validated accuracy, the same 0.812 as in the sweep above."],["search.best_estimator_","The refitted model, ready for <code>predict</code>."]],"tip":"Do not report best_score_ as your final accuracy: you chose C because it scored best on these folds. Keep a separate test set for the honest number."}],"check":[{"q":"You raise C from 0.1 to 100 on overlapping classes. What happens to the street and to the points inside it?","options":["The street widens so more points fit inside it","The street narrows and fewer points sit inside it","C sets the number of points allowed inside, so exactly 100 are allowed","The street does not change, because C only matters for separable data"],"answer":1,"why":"A larger C makes each unit of slack dearer, so the SVM shrinks the street and pushes more points outside it. On overlapping data this stops changing once C is large enough (the plateau)."},{"q":"A point has label y = −1 and the model scores it f(x) = +0.3. What is its slack ξ?","options":["0","0.7","1.3","0.3"],"answer":2,"why":"y·f = (−1)(0.3) = −0.3, which is negative: wrong side. ξ = max(0, 1 − (−0.3)) = 1.3. Using f alone instead of y·f gives the tempting but wrong 0.7."},{"q":"A linear SVM at C = 100 scores 100% training and 81% five-fold cross-validated accuracy, with scaled features. What do you try first?","options":["Remove the scaling","Pick the C with the best training accuracy","Lower C, for example 1 or 0.1, and compare cross-validated accuracy","Raise C to 10000 to remove the last errors"],"answer":2,"why":"A large gap between training and validation accuracy means the line is fitted to individual points. A smaller C widens the street and shrinks w. Choose the new C by cross-validation, never by training accuracy."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain what C does in a soft-margin SVM and how you would choose it.","model":"C is the price the SVM pays per unit a point breaks the street rule: a small C lets many points inside a wide street, a large C forces a narrow street that bends to individual points. I do not guess it: I try C on a log grid and pick from the broad best region of the cross-validated accuracy."},"cheat":"Soft margin: minimise ½‖w‖² + C·Σξ with slack ξ = max(0, 1 − y·f), the hinge loss. C is a price per unit of violation, not a count. Small C: wide street, many support vectors, tolerates errors. Large C: narrow street, fits single points. On overlapping data it plateaus. Choose C on a log grid by cross-validation."},"svm-margin/hinge-vs-log":{"idea":"A loss says how much to punish a point at margin m = y·f(x). The SVM's hinge loss is exactly zero once m reaches 1, so safe points are ignored. Logistic regression's log-loss never reaches zero, so every point keeps a small pull.","why":"Two models can draw almost the same line and still behave differently. Hinge loss lets only the few points near the street decide, which is why the SVM has support vectors. Log-loss lets every point speak, which is why logistic regression gives probabilities. Mix them up and you will read an SVM score as a probability.","analogy":"A tax office decides how closely to audit returns. One office ignores any return that is clearly in order and spends all its effort on borderline and wrong ones (hinge). Another reads every return, but spends less time the more obviously fine it looks, and never none (log-loss). Both catch the bad returns. Only the second is still nudged by the thousand tidy ones.","vocab":[["Margin m = y·f(x)","One number per point. Positive: correct side. Negative: wrong side. At m = 1 the point sits exactly on a street edge. Larger m means more confidently correct."],["Loss","The penalty one point adds, as a function of its margin. Training searches for the line with the smallest total penalty plus the ½‖w‖² term."],["0–1 loss","1 for a wrong point, 0 for a right one. It is what we care about, but it is flat except for one jump, so it gives an optimiser no direction."],["Hinge loss","<span class=\"eq\">max(0, 1 − m)</span>. Zero for m ≥ 1, then rises by 1 for each unit of margin lost. Same as the slack ξ of the soft margin."],["Log-loss","<span class=\"eq\">ln(1 + e<sup>−m</sup>)</span>. Smooth and always above zero. It is 0.693 at m = 0 and falls towards 0 as m grows."],["Pull (slope)","The slope of a point's loss at its margin. It is the strength with which the point drags the line. Slope 0 means no pull."],["Sigmoid σ(f)","<span class=\"eq\">1/(1 + e<sup>−f</sup>)</span>. Logistic regression squeezes the score f into 0 to 1 and calls it a probability. The SVM has no such step."]],"steps":[{"h":"What we want but cannot train","p":"We would like to minimise the number of mistakes: loss 1 if m &lt; 0, loss 0 otherwise. But that curve is flat everywhere except for one jump at m = 0. Nudging the line a little changes nothing until a point flips, so there is no slope to follow. We need a smooth stand-in that sits above it."},{"h":"Put every point on one axis","p":"Each point has a margin <span class=\"eq\">m = y·f(x)</span>. Its sign says which side it is on, and its size says how deep it is. Both losses are drawn against m, so a point at m = 2 gets the same loss whichever customer it is. Remember the landmarks: m = 0 on the boundary, m = 1 on a street edge."},{"h":"Hinge loss: a hockey stick","p":"<span class=\"eq\">max(0, 1 − m)</span>. Left of m = 1 it climbs at slope −1. From m = 1 on, it is flat at exactly 0. A point outside the street costs nothing and has no slope, so it cannot pull the line at all. A point at m = 0.5 costs 0.5 and pulls with full strength 1."},{"h":"Log-loss: a slide that never lands","p":"<span class=\"eq\">ln(1 + e<sup>−m</sup>)</span>. At m = 0 it is 0.693, at m = 1 it is 0.313, at m = 3 it is 0.049, and it never touches 0. Its slope is <span class=\"eq\">−1/(1 + e<sup>m</sup>)</span>: −0.269 at m = 1, −0.119 at m = 2, −0.047 at m = 3. Small, shrinking, never zero."},{"h":"Slope is pull","p":"The total loss is a sum, so its slope with respect to w is a sum of one term per point. Each term is the point's slope times its position. With hinge, every point at m ≥ 1 contributes 0: the line is set by the points in or beyond the street, the support vectors. With log-loss, every point contributes a little, the nearest most."},{"h":"Same recipe, different loss","p":"Both minimise <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C·Σ loss(mᵢ)</span>, and C means the same in both. So with the same C the two lines are close: 4.4° apart on the overlapping preset. Far on the wrong side, both losses grow like a straight line (slopes −1 and −0.92 at m = −2.5), so one wild point has a bounded pull."},{"h":"What each one gives you","p":"Hinge: a sparse set of support vectors and a line that ignores the crowd, but no probabilities. An SVM score of 2 does not mean 88%. Log-loss: a probability σ(f) and a line that every point nudges. With C tuned, accuracy is usually within noise. \"Easy\" means outside the street: at a tiny C the street is huge and even a correct point can still be inside it."}],"example":{"title":"six applicants, then one easy one","setup":"A trained line gives six applicants these margins <span class=\"eq\">m = y·f(x)</span>: <b>−1.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 3</b>. Compute the hinge loss and the log-loss of each, and the pull each one has on the line.","table":{"head":["m","Hinge max(0, 1 − m)","Log-loss ln(1 + e^−m)","Hinge pull","Log-loss pull 1/(1 + e^m)"],"rows":[["−1.5 (wrong side)","2.5","1.7014","1","0.8176"],["0 (on the boundary)","1.0","0.6931","1","0.5000"],["0.5 (inside the street)","0.5","0.4741","1","0.3775"],["1 (on the edge)","0","0.3133","0","0.2689"],["2 (outside)","0","0.1269","0","0.1192"],["3 (outside)","0","0.0486","0","0.0474"],["<b>Total</b>","<b>4.0</b>","<b>3.3574</b>","<b>3</b>","<b>2.1307</b>"]]},"walk":["Hinge: <span class=\"eq\">max(0, 1 − m)</span> gives 1 − (−1.5) = 2.5, then 1, then 0.5. At m = 1, 2 and 3 the bracket is zero or negative, so the loss is 0. Total <b>4.0</b>.","Log-loss: for m = 0.5, e<sup>−0.5</sup> = 0.6065, so ln(1.6065) = <b>0.4741</b>. For m = 3, e<sup>−3</sup> = 0.0498, so ln(1.0498) = <b>0.0486</b>. Small, but not zero. Total <b>3.3574</b>.","Pull = size of the slope. Hinge: 1 for the three points with m &lt; 1, and 0 for the others (at m = 1 exactly, the convention is 0). Log-loss: <span class=\"eq\">1/(1 + e<sup>m</sup>)</span>, which gives 0.8176, 0.5, 0.3775, 0.2689, 0.1192, 0.0474.","Now add one <b>easy applicant at m = 4</b>. Hinge: loss 0 and pull 0, so the SVM objective does not change and its line stays put. Log-loss: loss 0.0181 and pull 0.0180, so logistic regression still shifts a little.","One easy point pulls little, but the crowd adds up. A point at m = 0.5 has pull 0.3775. A point at m = 3 has 0.0474. If they sit in similar places, <b>8 points at m = 3</b> (0.3775 ÷ 0.0474) pull as hard as one point inside the street. For the hinge, no number of them would."],"result":"Hinge listens to 3 of the 6 points and ignores the rest, however many join. Log-loss listens to all 6 with weights from 0.82 down to 0.05. The three points with m ≥ 1 are half the points but only 0.4355 of the total log-loss pull of 2.1307."},"read":[["Upper plot: black solid curve","Hinge loss against the margin m. It reaches exactly 0 at m = 1, labelled \"m = 1\"."],["Upper plot: dashed curve in a second colour","Log-loss. It stays above zero for every m."],["Upper plot: grey dashed steps","The 0–1 loss: 1 on the left of m = 0, 0 on the right."],["Upper plot: vertical dashed line, round dot and square dot","Your slider value. The round dot sits on the hinge curve, the square dot on the log-loss curve."],["Panel \"At this margin\"","Five numbers for that m. \"Pull on the boundary\" is the slope: \"full (slope −1)\" or \"none (0)\" for the hinge, \"slope −…\" for the log-loss."],["Lower plot: solid black line with dashed street and rings","The SVM line, its street edges and its support vectors."],["Lower plot: dashed line in the second colour","The logistic regression line, fitted to the same points with the same C."],["Readout: Angle between the two lines","Angle between the two weight vectors. 0° means the same direction."],["Readout: Points that shape the SVM line","The support-vector count, as \"35 of 48\". Points inside the street and on the wrong side are included."],["Readouts: Last easy point, SVM line moved and logistic line moved","How far each line moved (in the units of a scale-free line description) when you last pressed the button. \"–\" until you press it. Values below 0.001 are solver tolerance, not real movement."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Drag \"Margin m\" to −1.00, then 1.00, then 3.00 and read the panel \"At this margin\".","see":"At −1.00: hinge 2.000, log-loss 1.313, pulls \"full (slope −1)\" and \"slope −0.731\". At 1.00: hinge 0.000, log-loss 0.313, pulls \"none (0)\" and \"slope −0.269\". At 3.00: hinge 0.000, log-loss 0.049, pull \"slope −0.047\".","why":"The hinge curve is flat from m = 1 onwards, so it has no slope there. The log-loss curve keeps falling and flattening but never reaches zero, so a small pull remains."},{"do":"Drag \"Margin m\" down to −2.5.","see":"Hinge loss 3.500 and log-loss 2.579, with pulls \"full (slope −1)\" and \"slope −0.924\". The two curves run almost parallel.","why":"Far on the wrong side both losses are close to straight lines of slope −1. A single wild point cannot pull harder than that, which makes both losses robust to outliers."},{"do":"Reload the page. With <i>Overlapping groups</i> and C = 1, press <i>Add an easy point far away</i> three times, reading \"Last easy point\" after each press.","see":"SVM line moved: 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001. Logistic line moved: 0.0113, 0.0093, 0.0077. \"Points that shape the SVM line\" goes 35 of 49, 35 of 50, 35 of 51.","why":"The new point lands outside the SVM street, where the hinge has slope 0. The SVM numbers are solver tolerance. The log-loss still has slope −1/(1 + e<sup>m</sup>) there, so the logistic line shifts far more (0.0113 against 0.0001)."},{"do":"Reload, drag C to the far left (0.01), then press <i>Add an easy point far away</i> once.","see":"The SVM line moves by 1.2916 and the logistic line by 0.2979. \"Points that shape the SVM line\" reads \"49 of 49\": every point is a support vector.","why":"At C = 0.01 the street is very wide, so the new point, though correct, is still inside it, where the hinge has slope −1. \"Easy\" means outside the street, not just correctly classified."},{"do":"Reload. At C = 1 read \"Angle between the two lines\" and \"Points that shape the SVM line\" on each of the three presets. Then, on <i>Two separated groups</i>, press <i>Add an easy point far away</i>.","see":"The angle is 4.4° (<i>Overlapping groups</i>, 35 of 48), 9.5° (<i>Two separated groups</i>, 7 of 28) and 9.9° (<i>Separated groups + one odd point</i>, 6 of 27). On the separated groups both lines then barely move (SVM 0.0002, logistic 0.0004).","why":"On well-separated data the added point is at m ≈ 6 for the logistic fit, where its pull is only 0.002. The pull falls like e<sup>−m</sup>. The two lines stay within 10° of each other. The SVM line is built from few points only when the data are clean: on overlapping data 35 of 48 are support vectors."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Hockey stick against a slide.</b> Hinge: flat floor from m = 1, then a straight stick up the left. Log-loss: a smooth slide that flattens towards the floor but never lands."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Both models minimise <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C × Σ loss(m)</span>. Swap the loss and you swap the model: hinge gives the SVM, log-loss gives logistic regression."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>Pull = slope.</b> Hinge slope: 1 below m = 1, then 0. Log-loss slope: <span class=\"eq\">1/(1 + e<sup>m</sup>)</span>, which is roughly e<sup>−m</sup> for large m, so it shrinks about 2.7 times per unit of margin."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"List <span class=\"eq\">y·f</span> for your points. Those with y·f &lt; 1 are the ones an SVM listens to. Their count is the support-vector count."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"An SVM score is a distance-like number, not a probability. For probabilities use logistic regression, or calibrate the SVM later (Session 6)."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>SVC(kernel=\"linear\")</code> uses the plain hinge, but <code>LinearSVC</code> uses the <b>squared</b> hinge by default. Pass <code>loss=\"hinge\"</code> to make them agree. Both <code>SVC</code> and <code>LogisticRegression</code> take the same <code>C</code>."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"Hinge loss is zero for every correctly classified point.\"","right":"Only for points with m ≥ 1, outside the street. A correct point at m = 0.5 still costs 0.5 and still pulls with strength 1."},{"wrong":"\"Log-loss ignores confident points.\"","right":"It shrinks their pull but never makes it zero. At m = 3 the pull is 0.047, which adds up over many easy points."},{"wrong":"\"An easy point can never move an SVM line.\"","right":"Only if it is outside the street. At C = 0.01 the street is so wide that a correct point can still be inside it, and then it moves the line by 1.29 in the widget."},{"wrong":"\"An SVM score of 2 means about 88% probability.\"","right":"The sigmoid of an SVM score is not a calibrated probability. Logistic regression is trained so that σ(f) estimates a probability. The SVM is trained only to get the side right with room to spare."}],"code":[{"title":"Hinge loss, log-loss and pull for six margins","py":"import numpy as np\n\nm = np.array([-1.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 3])          # margins y*f(x) of six points\nhinge = np.maximum(0, 1 - m)                    # SVM loss: exactly 0 once m >= 1\nlogloss = np.log1p(np.exp(-m))                  # logistic loss ln(1 + e^-m): never 0\npull = 1 / (1 + np.exp(m))                      # size of the log-loss slope at each m\nprint(\"hinge   \", hinge.round(4).tolist(), \"total\", round(float(hinge.sum()), 4))\nprint(\"log-loss\", logloss.round(4).tolist(), \"total\", round(float(logloss.sum()), 4))\nprint(\"log pull\", pull.round(4).tolist(), \"total\", round(float(pull.sum()), 4))\nprint(\"m = 4: hinge\", max(0, 1 - 4), \" log-loss\", round(float(np.log1p(np.exp(-4))), 4), \" log pull\", round(float(1 / (1 + np.exp(4))), 4))","out":"hinge    [2.5, 1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0] total 4.0\nlog-loss [1.7014, 0.6931, 0.4741, 0.3133, 0.1269, 0.0486] total 3.3574\nlog pull [0.8176, 0.5, 0.3775, 0.2689, 0.1192, 0.0474] total 2.1307\nm = 4: hinge 0  log-loss 0.0181  log pull 0.018","walk":[["np.maximum(0, 1 - m)","Compares 0 with <code>1 - m</code> for every element and keeps the larger. Negative values become 0, which is the flat part of the hinge."],["np.log1p(np.exp(-m))","<code>log1p(z)</code> is <code>log(1 + z)</code> computed accurately for small z. With z = e<sup>−m</sup> it is the log-loss."],["1 / (1 + np.exp(m))","The size of the log-loss slope, the \"pull\". It equals σ(−m), the probability the model gives to being wrong."],["hinge.round(4).tolist()","<code>round(4)</code> keeps 4 decimals; <code>tolist()</code> turns the array into a plain Python list so it prints cleanly."],["round(float(hinge.sum()), 4)","<code>sum()</code> adds the six losses. <code>float()</code> strips NumPy's type so <code>round</code> prints a plain number."]],"tip":"These are the numbers in the worked example: the m = 4 row shows that the hinge pull is exactly 0 while the log pull is 0.018."},{"title":"Add one easy point: which line moves?","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_blobs(n_samples=60, centers=[[-1, 0], [1, 0.5]], cluster_std=1.0, random_state=0)\nX2, y2 = np.vstack([X, [1.0, 1.0]]), np.append(y, 1)        # one easy class-1 point, just outside the SVM street\nline = lambda m: np.append(m.coef_[0], m.intercept_[0]) / np.linalg.norm(m.coef_[0])   # (w, b) / |w|\nfor model in [SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1), LogisticRegression(C=1)]:\n    before = line(model.fit(X, y))\n    after = line(model.fit(X2, y2))\n    print(type(model).__name__, \"moved by\", round(float(np.linalg.norm(after - before)), 3))","out":"SVC moved by 0.0\nLogisticRegression moved by 0.009","walk":[["np.vstack([X, [1.0, 1.0]])","Stacks a new row under the 60 rows. <code>np.append(y, 1)</code> adds its label, class 1."],["line = lambda m: ...","A one-line function. It joins <code>coef_[0]</code> (w) and <code>intercept_[0]</code> (b) and divides by ‖w‖, so lines that differ only by scale compare as equal."],["for model in [SVC(...), LogisticRegression(C=1)]","The same loop for both models. Both take <code>C</code> and mean the same by it."],["model.fit(X, y)","Trains on the 60 points and returns the model. <code>line(...)</code> reads w and b straight away, before the next fit overwrites them."],["np.linalg.norm(after - before)","The distance between the two scale-free lines. 0 means they are identical."]],"tip":"The point (1.0, 1.0) scores f = 1.38 on the SVM line: just outside the street, so the hinge ignores it. Put it at (0.5, 0.5) instead (f = 0.50, inside the street) and the SVM line moves by 0.047, more than the logistic line (0.026)."},{"title":"A score is not a probability","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_blobs(n_samples=60, centers=[[-1, 0], [1, 0.5]], cluster_std=1.0, random_state=0)\nlr, svm = LogisticRegression(C=1).fit(X, y), SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1).fit(X, y)\nx_new = [[2.0, 0.5]]\nf = lr.decision_function(x_new)[0]\nprint(\"logistic: f =\", round(float(f), 3), \" sigmoid(f) =\", round(float(1 / (1 + np.exp(-f))), 3), \" predict_proba =\", round(float(lr.predict_proba(x_new)[0, 1]), 3))\nprint(\"SVM: f =\", round(float(svm.decision_function(x_new)[0]), 3), \" has predict_proba?\", hasattr(svm, \"predict_proba\"))","out":"logistic: f = 3.017  sigmoid(f) = 0.953  predict_proba = 0.953\nSVM: f = 2.17  has predict_proba? False","walk":[["lr.decision_function(x_new)","The score f = w·x + b of the logistic model. The double brackets in <code>x_new</code> make it one row of two features."],["1 / (1 + np.exp(-f))","The sigmoid. For logistic regression, this equals <code>predict_proba</code>: the model was trained so that it is a probability."],["lr.predict_proba(x_new)[0, 1]","Row 0 (the only point), column 1 (the probability of class 1)."],["hasattr(svm, \"predict_proba\")","False: a plain <code>SVC</code> has no probability output. <code>SVC(probability=True)</code> adds a separate calibration step on top."]],"tip":"The SVM score 2.17 tells you the side and a distance-like confidence. Do not run it through a sigmoid and quote the result as a probability."}],"check":[{"q":"You add a correctly classified point far outside the street. Why does an SVM line stay put while a logistic regression line shifts slightly?","options":["The SVM ignores every correctly classified point","The SVM uses a smaller C","Logistic regression does not use a margin","The hinge slope is exactly 0 beyond m = 1, while the log-loss slope is small but never 0"],"answer":3,"why":"The hinge is flat from m = 1 on, so the point has no pull. The log-loss slope −1/(1 + e^m) is small but never 0. It is not true that the SVM ignores all correct points: a correct point inside the street (0 < m < 1) still has slope −1."},{"q":"A point has margin m = 0.5. What are its hinge loss and its log-loss (natural log)?","options":["0 and 0.474","0.5 and 0.5","0.5 and 0.474","1.5 and 0.974"],"answer":2,"why":"Hinge = max(0, 1 − 0.5) = 0.5. Log-loss = ln(1 + e^−0.5) = ln(1.6065) = 0.474. The point is correct but inside the street, so the hinge still charges it."},{"q":"You set C = 0.01 so the street becomes very wide, then add a correct point at f = 0.7, which lies inside this wide street. What happens to the SVM line?","options":["It stays, because SVM lines never respond to new points","It moves only if C is made large","It moves, because the point is inside the street and has hinge slope −1","It stays, because the point is correctly classified"],"answer":2,"why":"The hinge only forgets points with m ≥ 1. At f = 0.7, m = 0.7, so the point is inside the street and pulls with full strength. In the widget, an \"easy\" point at C = 0.01 moves the SVM line by 1.29."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain why an SVM ignores points far from the boundary but logistic regression does not.","model":"Both punish a point by its margin, but the hinge loss drops to exactly zero once a point is outside the street, so it has no slope and cannot pull the line. The log-loss only falls towards zero and never reaches it, so every point, even a very safe one, still gives the line a small push."},"cheat":"Margin m = y·f(x). Hinge max(0, 1 − m): zero for m ≥ 1, slope −1 below, so only points in or beyond the street pull (support vectors). Log-loss ln(1 + e^−m): slope −1/(1 + e^m), small but never 0, so every point pulls and σ(f) works as a probability. Same recipe ½‖w‖² + C·Σ loss, same C."},"svm-margin/scaling":{"idea":"An SVM measures the street as a distance and charges for the size of w, and both change with units. Put a useful feature in tiny numbers and the raw SVM stops using it. Standardise each feature, inside each training fold, and units stop mattering.","why":"Nobody agrees on units: rupees or lakh, metres or millimetres. If the model's answer changes when someone changes a unit, it is learning your spreadsheet's formatting, not your customers. In the simulation the same 60 points score 58% in tiny units and 87% after standardising.","analogy":"Two judges score a talent show. One marks out of 10, the other out of 1000, and you add the marks. The second judge decides every ranking, however poor their taste. Convert each judge's marks to \"how many standard deviations above that judge's average\" and both now count equally. That conversion is standardising.","vocab":[["Units of a feature","The scale you happened to record it in: ₹ or lakh, m or mm. They change the numbers, not the customers."],["Euclidean distance","The square root of the sum of squared differences, one term per feature. A feature with big numbers supplies most of the sum."],["Penalty ½‖w‖²","The cost the SVM charges for large weights. A weight needs to be larger when its feature has smaller numbers, so the penalty depends on units."],["Standardising (z-score)","<span class=\"eq\">z = (x − mean) / sd</span>, per column. The new column has mean 0 and standard deviation 1, in no unit at all."],["StandardScaler","The scikit-learn tool for it. <code>fit</code> learns each column's mean and sd, <code>transform</code> applies them. It divides by the population sd (n, not n − 1)."],["Pipeline","Steps chained into one model, <code>make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC())</code>. Inside cross-validation it refits every step on each training fold."],["Leakage","Test rows influencing the training in any way. Computing the mean and sd on all rows before splitting lets the test rows leak into the scaler."]],"steps":[{"h":"Units are arbitrary","p":"Income in rupees, in lakh or in dollars describes the same customers. A good model should give the same answers whichever you use. Unpenalised least squares and unpenalised logistic regression do: a change of unit only rescales the weight. The SVM does not, for two separate reasons."},{"h":"Reason 1: the margin is a distance","p":"The street width 2/‖w‖ is measured in the geometry of the features, and a squared distance is a sum <span class=\"eq\">(Δage)² + (Δincome)²</span>. Two customers 24 years and ₹53,000 apart are 99.99998% \"income\" in rupees. Express income in lakh and the same pair is 99.95% \"age\". Same customers, new unit, opposite dominant feature."},{"h":"Reason 2: the penalty is not unit-free","p":"The SVM minimises <span class=\"eq\">½‖w‖² + C·Σξ</span>. If you divide a feature by s, the same rule needs a weight s times larger, and the penalty grows by s². From rupees to lakh, s = 100,000: the weight grows 10⁵ times and the charge grows 10¹⁰ times. In rupees, income is nearly free to use. In lakh it is very dear."},{"h":"What the raw SVM does","p":"In the simulation at × 0.03, feature 1 has a spread of 0.0438. The standardised line, written in raw units, needs weight 42.4 on it and costs ½·42.4² = 899 to save only 34 of hinge loss (52.3 down to 18.0). The raw SVM refuses and keeps weight 1.75 (cost 1.7), leaning on the weak feature 2. Accuracy 58%, against 87%."},{"h":"The fix: standardise","p":"Replace each column by <span class=\"eq\">z = (x − mean)/sd</span>. Every feature now has spread 1, so no feature is cheap or dear because of its unit, and typical weights are near 1. Rupees and lakh give identical z-scores. That is why the standardised curve in the simulation is flat: it does not care about the multiplier."},{"h":"Fit the scaler on training rows only","p":"The mean and sd are learned from the rows you train on, then applied to everything. If you scale the whole dataset first and then cross-validate, the test rows helped to set the mean and sd: leakage. Put <code>StandardScaler</code> as the first step of a Pipeline and each fold refits it for itself. The simulation does exactly that."},{"h":"Limits to remember","p":"The damage is one-sided: with the informative feature in big units (× 100) the raw SVM is still fine, at 85% against 87%. On real data a linear SVM gains modestly, about 0.7 to 2.4 points on wine and breast cancer (repeated 5-fold cross-validation). The RBF kernel of Session 4 has no way to compensate, so it needs scaling more. TF-IDF rows have unit length already: do not centre sparse data."}],"example":{"title":"four customers, two units","setup":"Age in years and income in rupees for four customers. The same incomes are also written in lakh rupees (1 lakh = 100,000 ₹). Standardise with the population sd (divide by n = 4).","table":{"head":["Customer","Age","Income (₹)","Income (lakh)","z of age","z of income (₹ or lakh)"],"rows":[["1","28","32,000","0.32","−1.250","−1.197"],["2","35","48,000","0.48","−0.454","−0.364"],["3","41","55,000","0.55","0.227","0.000"],["4","52","85,000","0.85","1.477","1.561"],["<b>mean, sd</b>","39, 8.80","55,000, 19,222","0.55, 0.1922","0, 1","0, 1"]]},"walk":["Squared distance between customers 1 and 4, income in rupees: age gives 24² = 576, income gives 53,000² = 2,809,000,000. Income share = 2,809,000,000 / 2,809,000,576 = <b>99.99998%</b>. Age is invisible.","The same pair with income in lakh: age gives 576, income gives 0.53² = 0.2809. Age share = 576 / 576.2809 = <b>99.95%</b>. Now income is invisible. Only the unit changed.","Penalty. Suppose a rule scores one point per ₹20,000 of income, so w = 1/20,000 = 0.00005 per rupee. The cost ½w² = 1.25 × 10⁻⁹: free. In lakh the weight is 0.00005 × 100,000 = 5, and ½ × 5² = <b>12.5</b>. The same rule now costs ten billion times more.","Standardise income. Mean = 55,000. Deviations are −23,000, −7,000, 0, 30,000, squares sum to 1,478,000,000 and divide by 4 to give 369,500,000, so sd = <b>19,222</b>. Customer 1: (32,000 − 55,000)/19,222 = <b>−1.197</b>.","In lakh: mean 0.55, sd 0.19222, and (0.32 − 0.55)/0.19222 = <b>−1.197</b> again. The z-column is the same in either unit, so distances and weights built on it no longer depend on the unit."],"result":"Changing rupees to lakh swapped the dominant feature in the distance (income 99.99998% to age 99.95%) and multiplied the price of using income by 10¹⁰. After standardising, both units give the identical column −1.197, −0.364, 0, 1.561."},"read":[["Slider \"Multiply feature 1 by\"","Changes the units of feature 1 only. × 0.03 makes its numbers 33 times smaller (a bigger unit: metres instead of millimetres). It starts at × 0.03. Feature 2 never changes."],["Left plot \"Raw units: what the unscaled SVM sees\"","The 60 points in raw numbers with the raw SVM's line. Look at the tick labels on the horizontal axis: they get tiny as the multiplier falls."],["Right plot \"After standardising\"","The same points with both features rescaled to mean 0 and sd 1, and the standardised SVM's line."],["Blue circles and orange triangles","Class A and class B. Feature 1 (horizontal) separates them well. Feature 2 (vertical) barely does."],["Bottom plot: solid and dashed curves","Cross-validated accuracy against the multiplier (log scale). Solid is standardised, dashed is raw. The vertical dashed line is your multiplier."],["Readout: Raw SVM, cross-validated accuracy","5-fold accuracy of an SVM on the raw numbers, C = 1."],["Readout: Standardised SVM, cross-validated accuracy","The same, but the scaler is refitted on each training fold."],["Readouts: share of the score from feature 1","<span class=\"eq\">|w₁|·sd₁ / (|w₁|·sd₁ + |w₂|·sd₂)</span>: how much of the spread of the score comes from feature 1. Near 100% means feature 2 is ignored."],["Button \"New data\"","Draws a fresh sample of 60 points. The pattern should repeat on each."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Look at the starting state (× 0.03), including the left plot.","see":"Raw 58% against standardised 87%. Raw share of the score from feature 1 is 11%, standardised 90%. In the left plot the black line is nearly flat (under 10° from horizontal) and cuts through both clouds, though the blue points sit mostly on the left and the orange ones mostly on the right.","why":"The raw SVM found it too costly to put weight on a feature with tiny numbers, so it separates mostly with feature 2, which is only weakly informative."},{"do":"Drag the multiplier to the far left (× 0.01).","see":"Raw falls to 53%, close to a coin toss, and its share from feature 1 to 1%. Standardised stays at 87%.","why":"Feature 1 now has a spread of only 0.015, so the weight that would use it costs even more. Standardising removes the multiplier completely, so its row never moves."},{"do":"Drag the multiplier up to about × 0.3.","see":"Raw reaches 87%, equal to the standardised value, and its share from feature 1 is 91%. The black line in the left plot is now nearly vertical.","why":"At × 0.3 feature 1 has spread 0.44, close to feature 2's 0.96, so the raw SVM can afford a good weight on it. The raw and standardised models become almost the same model."},{"do":"Drag the multiplier to the far right (× 100). Then look at the bottom plot.","see":"Raw is 85% and standardised 87%: the raw SVM does not collapse. In the curve, raw is far below standardised only for multipliers below about 0.06, and within 4 points of it from about 0.1 upwards.","why":"Large numbers on the <i>informative</i> feature make it nearly free to use, so the SVM leans on it, which happens to be right. The harm is one-sided: it comes when the useful feature is in small numbers (or a useless one in big ones)."},{"do":"Press <i>New data</i> four times, reading both accuracies at × 0.03 each time.","see":"Raw is 67%, 60%, 62% and 60%, standardised 85%, 85%, 78% and 83%. At about × 0.3 raw is within 1 to 4 points of standardised on each sample.","why":"It is the scale, not luck in one sample. With 60 points, one point is 1.7% accuracy, so 1 or 2 points of difference is noise but 20 or more is not."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Same customers, different ruler.</b> Rupees and lakh are two rulers. The SVM measures the street with whichever ruler you hand it. Standardising hands every feature the same ruler: one standard deviation per tick."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Any method that uses distances or a penalty on weights (SVM, k-NN, PCA, ridge, logistic with a penalty) needs standardised features. Trees do not."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Divide a feature by s and its weight multiplies by s while its penalty multiplies by <b>s²</b>. Units enter the cost <i>squared</i>."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Print <code>X.std(axis=0)</code>. If the largest spread is more than about 10 times the smallest, scale. After scaling, the training columns should show mean 0 and sd 1."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"If rescaling does not change the accuracy, do not conclude \"scaling is useless\". For a linear SVM the gain is modest, but the best C moves with the scale and the RBF kernel (Session 4) does need it."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC(...))</code>. <code>fit</code> learns mean and sd from the rows it is given, <code>transform</code> applies them. In cross-validation, the Pipeline refits the scaler inside each training fold."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"SVMs are scale-invariant, like decision trees.\"","right":"Trees only compare one feature with a cut point, so units do not matter. An SVM adds squared differences and penalises ‖w‖², so units matter twice."},{"wrong":"\"Scale the whole dataset first, then cross-validate.\"","right":"The test rows then help set the mean and sd: leakage. Fit the scaler on training rows only, which a Pipeline does for you in every fold."},{"wrong":"\"Big numbers always hurt and small numbers are always safe.\"","right":"The feature in big numbers <i>dominates</i>. That hurts only if it is not the useful one. In the simulation, × 100 on the informative feature costs just 2 points (85% against 87%), while × 0.03 on it costs 28."},{"wrong":"\"Standardise TF-IDF text features as well.\"","right":"TF-IDF rows already have length 1, so features are comparable. Centring sparse data makes it dense. If you must rescale, use <code>StandardScaler(with_mean=False)</code> or <code>MaxAbsScaler</code>."}],"code":[{"title":"Raw against scaled SVM as the unit of feature 1 changes","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nrng, y = np.random.RandomState(0), np.array([-1, 1] * 30)\nX = np.column_stack([0.9 * y + 0.9 * rng.randn(60), 0.3 * y + rng.randn(60)])   # feature 1 strong, 2 weak\nscaled = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC(kernel=\"linear\"))\nfor k in [0.01, 0.03, 0.3, 1, 100]:                    # k = new units for feature 1 only\n    a, b = (cross_val_score(m, X * [k, 1], y, cv=5).mean() for m in (SVC(kernel=\"linear\"), scaled))\n    print(f\"x{k:<5} raw={a:.3f} scaled={b:.3f}\")","out":"x0.01  raw=0.667 scaled=0.850\nx0.03  raw=0.700 scaled=0.850\nx0.3   raw=0.850 scaled=0.850\nx1     raw=0.867 scaled=0.850\nx100   raw=0.850 scaled=0.850","walk":[["np.array([-1, 1] * 30)","Sixty alternating labels. <code>0.9 * y</code> pushes the two classes to −0.9 and +0.9 on feature 1, so it carries the signal. Feature 2 gets only 0.3 * y, so it is weak."],["rng.randn(60)","Sixty draws from a standard normal. <code>RandomState(0)</code> fixes the seed so every run prints the same numbers."],["X * [k, 1]","NumPy broadcasting: column 0 is multiplied by k and column 1 by 1. It changes the unit of feature 1 only, which is what the slider does."],["make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC(...))","Two steps in one model: scale, then SVM. <code>cross_val_score</code> clones it for each fold, so the scaler sees training rows only."],["cross_val_score(m, X * [k, 1], y, cv=5).mean()","Five fits, each tested on the fold it did not see. <code>.mean()</code> is the cross-validated accuracy."]],"tip":"The scaled column is the same in every row (0.850) because standardising cancels the multiplier exactly. The raw column moves."},{"title":"Why the raw SVM refuses: the same line costs far more in raw units","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nrng, y = np.random.RandomState(0), np.array([-1, 1] * 30)\nX = np.column_stack([0.9 * y + 0.9 * rng.randn(60), 0.3 * y + rng.randn(60)]) * [0.03, 1]   # feature 1 in tiny units\nscaler = StandardScaler().fit(X)\nw_std = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1).fit(scaler.transform(X), y).coef_[0]\nw_raw = w_std / scaler.scale_                          # the same line, written in raw units\nprint(\"column spreads:\", scaler.scale_.round(4))\nprint(\"w in z-units:\", w_std.round(2), \" penalty 0.5*|w|^2 =\", round(float(0.5 * w_std @ w_std), 2))\nprint(\"same line in raw units:\", w_raw.round(1), \" penalty =\", round(float(0.5 * w_raw @ w_raw), 1))","out":"column spreads: [0.0393 1.1066]\nw in z-units: [1.52 0.8 ]  penalty 0.5*|w|^2 = 1.48\nsame line in raw units: [38.7  0.7]  penalty = 747.8","walk":[["StandardScaler().fit(X)","Learns one mean and one standard deviation per column. <code>scale_</code> holds the standard deviations: here 0.0393 for feature 1 and 1.1066 for feature 2."],["scaler.transform(X)","Applies <span class=\"eq\">(x − mean) / sd</span> to every entry. This is the data the SVM was trained on."],[".coef_[0]","The learned weights, in z-units: the first row of the 2-D array <code>coef_</code>."],["w_std / scaler.scale_","A weight on a z-score is a weight divided by the sd on the raw number, because <code>z = (x − mean) / sd</code>. So the same line has raw weights 38.7 and 0.7."],["0.5 * w_std @ w_std","½‖w‖²: <code>@</code> is the dot product of a vector with itself, the sum of squares."]],"tip":"The identical line costs 1.48 in z-units and 747.8 in raw units. A raw SVM weighing 747.8 against a hinge-loss saving of a few dozen will choose the cheap, wrong line."},{"title":"Standardising by hand, and the same answer in rupees and lakh","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nage = np.array([28, 35, 41, 52.0])\nincome = np.array([32000, 48000, 55000, 85000.0])               # rupees\nX = np.column_stack([age, income])\nz_rupee = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)\nz_lakh = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X * [1, 1e-5])          # income in lakh rupees instead\nprint(\"z of income, rupees:\", z_rupee[:, 1].round(3))\nprint(\"z of income, lakh:  \", z_lakh[:, 1].round(3))\nprint(\"by hand (x - mean)/std:\", ((income - income.mean()) / income.std()).round(3))","out":"z of income, rupees: [-1.197 -0.364  0.     1.561]\nz of income, lakh:   [-1.197 -0.364  0.     1.561]\nby hand (x - mean)/std: [-1.197 -0.364  0.     1.561]","walk":[["np.column_stack([age, income])","Joins two 1-D arrays as the two columns of a 4 × 2 table: rows are customers, columns are features."],["fit_transform(X)","<code>fit</code> learns mean and sd per column, then <code>transform</code> applies them, in one call. Use it on training data only; for new data call <code>transform</code> alone."],["X * [1, 1e-5]","Multiplies the age column by 1 and the income column by 0.00001, which converts rupees to lakh."],["z_rupee[:, 1]","All rows (<code>:</code>) of column 1, the income column."],["income.std()","NumPy's <code>std</code> divides by n (population sd), which is what <code>StandardScaler</code> uses. Pandas' <code>.std()</code> divides by n − 1 and would give slightly different z-scores."]],"tip":"Identical z-columns for rupees and lakh: this is the whole reason standardising makes the SVM unit-free."}],"check":[{"q":"Why does a raw linear SVM under-use a feature that is measured in very small numbers?","options":["Using it needs a large weight, and the ‖w‖² penalty makes large weights expensive","The hinge loss is zero for small numbers","Small numbers cannot lie on the street edge","SVMs delete features whose values are small"],"answer":0,"why":"To turn small numbers into a decisive score the weight must be big. The SVM pays ½‖w‖² for it, so it often prefers to lean on features with larger numbers."},{"q":"A rule needs weight w = 0.00004 per rupee of income. You switch the income column to lakh rupees (1 lakh = 100,000 ₹). What weight does the same rule need now, and what does ½w² become?","options":["w = 0.4 and ½w² = 0.08","w = 4 and ½w² = 16","w = 0.00004 and ½w² = 8 × 10⁻¹⁰","w = 4 and ½w² = 8"],"answer":3,"why":"One lakh is 100,000 times as many rupees, so the weight per lakh is 0.00004 × 100,000 = 4. The penalty ½ × 4² = 8. In rupees it was ½ × 0.00004² = 8 × 10⁻¹⁰."},{"q":"You standardise the whole dataset with StandardScaler and only then run 5-fold cross-validation, instead of putting the scaler in a Pipeline. What is wrong?","options":["The SVM will refuse to train on scaled data","Nothing: scaling is not learned from the data","The mean will be 0 but the sd will not be 1","The test rows helped set the mean and sd, so the cross-validated score can be optimistic"],"answer":3,"why":"The scaler learns a mean and sd from data. If that data includes the test fold, the test rows have leaked into training. A Pipeline refits the scaler inside each training fold."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why an SVM needs scaled features and how you add the scaling safely.","model":"An SVM measures the street as a distance and pays for the size of w, and both depend on the units of each feature, so a feature in tiny numbers needs a huge, expensive weight and gets ignored. I standardise every feature to mean 0 and sd 1, and I do it inside a Pipeline so the mean and sd come from the training fold only."},"cheat":"The SVM margin is a Euclidean distance and the penalty is ½‖w‖², and neither is unit-free: divide a feature by s and its weight cost grows by s². A useful feature in tiny numbers gets ignored (58% against 87%). Fix: z = (x − mean)/sd on each column, fitted on training rows only, with make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), SVC(...))."},"svm-margin/text":{"idea":"Turn each message into a TF-IDF vector, one number per word, then fit a linear SVM. The model is one weight per word: a message's score is the sum of weight × TF-IDF over its words, plus an offset. The sign is the class.","why":"Ticket routing, spam filters and news sorting all start with text, which has thousands of words and few examples. A linear SVM copes well with that, and you can read it: every word gets a weight. Misread it and you will trust a perfect training score, take a weight for a cause, or expect a word the model never saw to count.","analogy":"A mail-room clerk sorts letters into a billing tray and a technical tray. The clerk has a notebook with one number per word: \"invoice −1.2\", \"error +0.9\". For each letter the clerk adds up the numbers of the words found in it (rare words count more), adds a small standing bias, and checks the sign. A word that is not in the notebook adds nothing.","vocab":[["Vocabulary","The list of distinct words seen in the training messages. Each word is one feature (one column). Here 198 words with common words dropped, 245 with them kept."],["TF-IDF","tf × idf for each word, then the message scaled to length 1. tf is how often the word appears in the message. <span class=\"eq\">idf = ln((1+N)/(1+df)) + 1</span>, where df is the number of messages containing the word: rarer words score higher."],["Stop words","Very common words (the, a, and, my) that say little about the topic. The page can drop them with a list."],["Weight wⱼ","One number per word. Negative pushes a message towards billing, positive towards technical. A larger size means a stronger cue."],["Offset b","The score of a message with no known words. It is small here, about 0.05."],["Unseen word","A word that is not in the training vocabulary. It has no column and no weight, so it is ignored."],["Hold-out set","12 messages kept out of training and used only to test. One message is 8.3 points of accuracy."]],"steps":[{"h":"Words are not numbers yet","p":"A classifier needs a table of numbers. Build a vocabulary from the training messages, then give each message one entry per word: how many times it appears. Order is ignored (a \"bag of words\"). Here 48 messages and 198 words give a 48 × 198 table that is almost all zeros."},{"h":"Raw counts mislead","p":"Common words appear everywhere and tell you nothing, and long messages get bigger numbers. TF-IDF fixes both. Multiply each count by <span class=\"eq\">idf = ln((1+N)/(1+df)) + 1</span>: a word in 1 of 48 messages gets 4.20, one in 5 gets 3.10. Then scale each message to length 1, so a long message and a short one are comparable."},{"h":"One weight per word","p":"The model is <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = Σ wⱼ·xⱼ + b</span>, with j running over the words. Positive f means technical, negative means billing. Training finds w by the same widest-street rule as the 2-feature page, now in 198 dimensions. Nothing else changes: the street is 2/‖w‖ and f = ±1 are its edges."},{"h":"Reading one prediction","p":"Only the words present have a non-zero xⱼ, so the score is a short sum: for each word in the message, weight × TF-IDF, then add b. The largest terms in size decide the call. A word never seen in training has no column, so it contributes exactly 0. A sentence of unseen words gets the offset alone."},{"h":"More words than messages","p":"With 198 words and 48 messages there is always a line that separates the training set, so training accuracy is 100% at every C. At C = 1000 every message has y·f = 1.000: all 48 sit exactly on a street edge, so all 48 are support vectors. A perfect training score here is not evidence of anything."},{"h":"Judge on messages the model has not seen","p":"Hold-out accuracy is the honest number, but with 12 messages one message is 8.3 points: 75% against 83% is a single message. A real project needs far more text, and chooses C and settings with cross-validation (live Step 6), not by looking at 12 messages."},{"h":"What C does here","p":"At C = 0.01 every message is inside the street (y·f is between 0.008 and 0.021), so every message sits at its cap and the weights are just C times a sum of message vectors. They scale with C: the biggest weight is 0.02 at C = 0.01 and 1.33 at C = 1. From about C = 2 up nothing changes: all 48 messages sit exactly on a street edge. Hold-out stays 75% throughout."},{"h":"Weights show what the model uses, not what is true","p":"With common words dropped, the top weights are topical (payment, invoice, error, page). Keep them, and <i>and</i>, <i>when</i>, <i>can</i> and <i>please</i> climb the list. With only 48 messages the SVM uses whatever separates them, even by accident. Use the weights to inspect the model and to find bugs, not to explain the world."}],"example":{"title":"one message, scored word by word","setup":"The page at its default settings: C = 1, common words dropped, 48 training messages. Score the sentence <b>\"Please send a refund for my last payment\"</b> using the learned weights shown in the table.","table":{"head":["Word","df (of 48 messages)","idf = ln(49/(1+df)) + 1","TF-IDF (length 1)","Weight w","w × TF-IDF"],"rows":[["send","1","4.1987","0.6268","−0.4346","−0.272"],["refund","1","4.1987","0.6268","−0.4472","−0.280"],["payment","5","3.1001","0.4628","−1.3348","−0.618"],["offset b","","","","","+0.046"],["<b>Score</b>","","","","","<b>−1.124</b>"]]},"walk":["Split into lower-case words of two or more letters: please, send, refund, for, my, last, payment. Drop the stop words please, for, my, last (and the one-letter \"a\"). Left: <b>send, refund, payment</b>.","idf = ln((1 + 48)/(1 + df)) + 1. \"send\" and \"refund\" each occur in 1 training message: ln(49/2) + 1 = <b>4.1987</b>. \"payment\" occurs in 5: ln(49/6) + 1 = <b>3.1001</b>. Each word appears once, so tf = 1 and the raw vector is (4.1987, 4.1987, 3.1001).","Scale to length 1: √(4.1987² + 4.1987² + 3.1001²) = √44.869 = 6.6985. So send = 4.1987/6.6985 = <b>0.6268</b>, refund = 0.6268, payment = 3.1001/6.6985 = <b>0.4628</b>. The squares add up to 1.","Multiply by the learned weights: −1.3348 × 0.4628 = −0.618 for payment, −0.4472 × 0.6268 = −0.280 for refund, −0.4346 × 0.6268 = −0.272 for send. These are the numbers in the page's breakdown table.","Add the offset 0.046: <span class=\"eq\">−0.618 − 0.280 − 0.272 + 0.046 = −1.124</span>. Negative means <b>billing</b>. |score| is above 1, so the message is outside the street: a confident call.","Now a mixed sentence, \"I was charged twice for the subscription and the app crashed\". Only <i>subscription</i> (weight −0.4237, TF-IDF 0.742, giving −0.314) and <i>app</i> (+0.5107, 0.670, giving +0.342) are in the vocabulary. <i>charged</i>, <i>twice</i> and <i>crashed</i> are ignored. Score = −0.314 + 0.342 + 0.046 = <b>+0.074</b>."],"result":"The first sentence is billing with score −1.124, led by \"payment\". The second is called technical with score +0.074: inside the street, so barely a decision, because the two strongest billing cues (charged, twice) were never seen in training. Only words in the vocabulary can vote."},"read":[["Slider C and tick box \"Drop common words (stop-word list)\"","The two settings of the model. Changing either refits the SVM on the 48 training messages."],["Readout: Vocabulary","The number of distinct words (columns) seen in training: 198 with common words dropped, 245 with them kept."],["Readouts: Training accuracy (48 messages), Hold-out accuracy (12 messages)","Accuracy on messages the model was trained on and on 12 it never saw. One hold-out message is 8.3 points."],["Readout: Support vectors","Written as \"48 of 48\": the training messages on or inside the street."],["Four buttons under \"Try a sentence\"","Fill the box below with an example. You can also type your own."],["Panel \"Your sentence\": prediction line","The predicted class, the score f, and whether it is \"outside\" or \"inside\" the street (the street ends at ±1)."],["Panel \"Your sentence\": table","One row per known word: weight × TF-IDF, with a bar. Blue-side bars push to billing, orange-side bars to technical. The last row is the offset."],["Line \"Ignored (never seen in training)\"","Words in your sentence that have no column. They contribute nothing."],["Panel \"What the SVM learned\"","The 10 largest negative weights (towards billing) and 10 largest positive weights (towards technical). Bars are scaled to the largest weight."],["Panel \"Hold-out messages\"","The 12 test messages, their true and predicted class and score. Wrong ones are red with a ✗."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Press the first sample button, \"Please send a refund for my last payment\", with C = 1 and common words dropped.","see":"Prediction billing, score −1.12, \"outside the street\". Rows: payment −0.618, refund −0.280, send −0.272, offset 0.046.","why":"This is the worked example. The sum of the rows equals the score, and the word with the highest TF-IDF-weighted weight, \"payment\", decides."},{"do":"Press the third sample button, \"I was charged twice for the subscription and the app crashed\".","see":"Prediction technical, score 0.07, \"inside the street\". Rows: app +0.342, subscription −0.314. A line says \"Ignored (never seen in training): charged, twice, crashed\".","why":"The two words that would have pulled hardest towards billing were not in the 48 training messages. The model decides on the two words it knows, and the result is a near tie."},{"do":"Press the fourth sample button, \"banana pyjamas\". Then drag C to the far left (0.01).","see":"At C = 1: \"No known words in this sentence, so only the offset (0.05) decides\", prediction technical, score 0.05. At C = 0.01 the offset reads −0.00 and the prediction flips to billing.","why":"With no known words the score is the offset, and its sign is a leftover of the fit. Its size is nearly zero, so the call is a coin toss. A low score near 0 means \"no evidence\", not \"technical\"."},{"do":"Untick \"Drop common words (stop-word list)\" and read the readouts and the weight lists.","see":"Vocabulary goes from 198 to 245 and hold-out accuracy from 75% to 83%. The technical list now starts \"and 0.78, when 0.75, error 0.73, in 0.73\" and the billing list has \"can −0.91, for −0.89, please −0.64, was −0.61\".","why":"Words like <i>and</i> and <i>can</i> happen to fall more on one side in 48 messages, and the SVM uses them. The gain of one hold-out message (8 points) is noise, not a sign that stop words help."},{"do":"Drag C from 1 down to 0.01, then up to 1000, watching the weight lists and the readouts.","see":"At C = 0.01 the largest weights are 0.02 and −0.02 (against 1.33 and −1.33 at C = 1), but training stays 100%, hold-out stays 75% and support vectors stay \"48 of 48\". At C = 1000 the largest weights are again about 1.33 and −1.33, as at C = 1.","why":"At small C every message is inside the street and only the size of w shrinks, not its direction. From about C = 2 up the SVM is already a hard margin, so a stricter C cannot change the answer."},{"do":"Type \"I think there is an extra fee on my bill that I do not understand.\" into the box. It is one of the hold-out messages.","see":"Prediction technical, score 0.14. The only known word is \"not\" (+0.092). The ignored line lists think, extra, fee, bill, understand. The hold-out table shows the same message in red with ✗.","why":"A billing message is called technical because the model knows only the word \"not\", which appears in error messages. The words that mark it as billing, \"fee\" and \"bill\", never occurred in training."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>A clerk's notebook, one number per word.</b> Add up the entries for the words in the letter, add the bias, and look at the sign. Words not in the notebook add nothing."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Score = Σ (weight × TF-IDF) over the words present + offset. Sign gives the class, and |score| ≥ 1 means outside the street."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"The rows of the breakdown table plus the offset must add up to the score shown. If your sentence has ignored words, they are in the \"never seen\" line and contribute 0."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"When there are more features than examples, a linear model fits the training set perfectly. Ignore training accuracy and judge on held-out data or cross-validation."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"TF-IDF in one line: <b>count × rarity, then scale to length 1</b>. A word in few messages has high rarity (idf). The length-1 step stops long messages winning by size."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>tfidf.fit_transform(train_texts)</code> learns the vocabulary and idf from training text only, then <code>tfidf.transform(new_text)</code> reuses them. Put both steps in a Pipeline. <code>svm.coef_</code> lines up with <code>tfidf.get_feature_names_out()</code>."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"A big weight means the word causes the class.\"","right":"It means the SVM used the word to separate these 48 messages. With common words kept, \"and\" is one of the biggest technical weights. Weights are a view of the model, not of the world."},{"wrong":"\"100% training accuracy means the model is good.\"","right":"With 198 words and 48 messages, it is 100% at every C. The hold-out score is 75%, and 3 of its 12 messages are wrong."},{"wrong":"\"Hold-out accuracy rose from 75% to 83%, so dropping stop words hurts.\"","right":"It is one message out of 12. Differences of 1 or 2 messages are noise on a test set this small."},{"wrong":"\"Scale the TF-IDF columns with StandardScaler, as in the scaling page.\"","right":"Each TF-IDF row already has length 1, so the features are comparable. Centring sparse data would also make it dense. If you must rescale, use <code>MaxAbsScaler</code> or <code>StandardScaler(with_mean=False)</code>."}],"code":[{"title":"Build TF-IDF by hand and check it","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\ndocs = [\"refund my payment\", \"invoice for payment\", \"billing plan\", \"card payment declined\", \"app shows error\", \"page is blank\", \"app crashes on phone\", \"cannot open page\"]\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\")        # lower-cases, drops my/for/is/on, keeps the rest\nX = tfidf.fit_transform(docs)                         # sparse table: 8 messages x vocabulary\nwords = list(tfidf.get_feature_names_out())\nprint(\"shape:\", X.shape, \" words:\", words[:5], \"...\")\nprint(\"idf(payment): sklearn\", round(float(tfidf.idf_[words.index(\"payment\")]), 4), \" by hand\", round(float(np.log((1 + 8) / (1 + 3)) + 1), 4))\nrow = X[0].toarray()[0]                               # message 0: \"refund my payment\"\nprint({w: round(float(v), 3) for w, v in zip(words, row) if v > 0}, \" length\", round(float(np.linalg.norm(row)), 3))","out":"shape: (8, 15)  words: ['app', 'billing', 'blank', 'card', 'crashes'] ...\nidf(payment): sklearn 1.8109  by hand 1.8109\n{'payment': 0.586, 'refund': 0.81}  length 1.0","walk":[["TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\")","Does tokenising, lower-casing, stop-word removal and TF-IDF in one object. It uses the same formula as the page: smooth idf and unit-length rows."],["tfidf.fit_transform(docs)","<code>fit</code> learns the vocabulary and each word's idf from these messages. <code>transform</code> turns the messages into the table. The result is <b>sparse</b>: only non-zero entries are stored."],["tfidf.get_feature_names_out()","The words in column order. Column j of the table and entry j of <code>svm.coef_</code> both refer to word j."],["np.log((1 + 8) / (1 + 3)) + 1","The idf formula by hand: N = 8 messages, \"payment\" in df = 3 of them, so ln(9/4) + 1 = 1.8109."],["X[0].toarray()[0]","Row 0 as a plain dense array, so we can print it. Its length is 1.0 because of the L2 scaling."]],"tip":"Use <code>fit_transform</code> on training text only, and <code>transform</code> on anything new. The idf must not come from test messages."},{"title":"Fit a linear SVM and read the weights","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\ndocs = [\"refund my payment\", \"invoice for payment\", \"billing plan\", \"card payment declined\", \"app shows error\", \"page is blank\", \"app crashes on phone\", \"cannot open page\"]\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\")\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1).fit(tfidf.fit_transform(docs), [0] * 4 + [1] * 4)   # 0 billing, 1 technical\nw, words = svm.coef_.toarray()[0], tfidf.get_feature_names_out()                    # one weight per word\nfor i in np.argsort(w)[[0, 1, -2, -1]]:               # two most negative and two most positive weights\n    print(f\"{words[i]:<8} {w[i]:+.2f}\")\nprint(\"offset b =\", round(float(svm.intercept_[0]), 3), \" support vectors:\", len(svm.support_))","out":"payment  -1.04\nbilling  -0.71\napp      +0.79\npage     +0.89\noffset b = 0.022  support vectors: 8","walk":[["[0] * 4 + [1] * 4","The labels: four billing messages (0) then four technical (1). Class 1 is the positive side, so positive weights point to technical."],["svm.coef_.toarray()[0]","For sparse input <code>coef_</code> is a sparse 1 × words matrix. <code>toarray()</code> makes it dense and <code>[0]</code> takes its only row: one weight per word."],["np.argsort(w)[[0, 1, -2, -1]]","<code>argsort</code> gives word positions from smallest to largest weight. Positions 0 and 1 are the most negative, −2 and −1 the most positive."],["f\"{words[i]:<8} {w[i]:+.2f}\"","Format: word left-aligned in 8 characters, then the weight with a sign and 2 decimals."],["len(svm.support_)","8 of 8 messages are support vectors: more words (15) than messages (8), so every message sits on the street, as on the page."]],"tip":"Weights are negative for billing and positive for technical only because you labelled billing 0 and technical 1. Swap the labels and every sign flips."},{"title":"Score a new sentence, word by word","py":"from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\ndocs = [\"refund my payment\", \"invoice for payment\", \"billing plan\", \"card payment declined\", \"app shows error\", \"page is blank\", \"app crashes on phone\", \"cannot open page\"]\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words=\"english\")\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1).fit(tfidf.fit_transform(docs), [0] * 4 + [1] * 4)\nx = tfidf.transform([\"refund the payment, the app crashed\"])     # \"crashed\" was never seen, so it is ignored\nparts, words = x.multiply(svm.coef_).toarray()[0], tfidf.get_feature_names_out()   # weight x tf-idf per word\nprint({words[i]: round(float(parts[i]), 3) for i in parts.nonzero()[0]})\nprint(\"sum + b =\", round(float(parts.sum() + svm.intercept_[0]), 3), \" decision_function =\", round(float(svm.decision_function(x)[0]), 3))\nprint(\"predicted:\", [\"billing\", \"technical\"][svm.predict(x)[0]])","out":"{'app': 0.445, 'payment': -0.507, 'refund': -0.339}\nsum + b = -0.379  decision_function = -0.379\npredicted: billing","walk":[["tfidf.transform([...])","Uses the vocabulary and idf learned earlier. Unknown words such as \"crashed\" have no column and are dropped. The brackets make it a list with one message."],["x.multiply(svm.coef_)","Element-wise product of the message's TF-IDF row and the weights: the <code>weight × TF-IDF</code> of every word, as in the page's breakdown table."],["parts.nonzero()[0]","The positions of the words that are actually present. All others contribute 0."],["parts.sum() + svm.intercept_[0]","The score: sum of contributions plus the offset b. It equals <code>decision_function</code> exactly, which is the check."],["svm.predict(x)[0]","The class, 0 or 1: just the sign of the score. Used as an index into the list of names."]],"tip":"If your hand sum and <code>decision_function</code> differ, you probably forgot the offset or used a different vectoriser on the new text."}],"check":[{"q":"In a linear SVM on TF-IDF features, how is a message's score computed?","options":["The distance to the nearest training message","The number of billing words minus the number of technical words","The sum, over the words it contains, of weight × TF-IDF, plus the offset","The probability that the message is technical"],"answer":2,"why":"The model is f(x) = Σ wⱼxⱼ + b. Words that are absent have xⱼ = 0 and drop out. The score is not a probability and not a word count."},{"q":"A message has two known words with weights −1.2 and +0.5, TF-IDF values 0.6 and 0.8, and the offset is b = 0.1. What are the score and the prediction (negative = billing)?","options":["−0.22, billing, inside the street","−0.32, billing","−0.62, billing","+0.22, technical"],"answer":0,"why":"(−1.2)(0.6) = −0.72 and (0.5)(0.8) = +0.40. The sum is −0.32 and adding the offset gives −0.22. It is negative, so billing, and |−0.22| is below 1, so it is inside the street."},{"q":"A sentence contains only words that were never in the training messages. What decides the prediction?","options":["The most common word in the sentence","The class that had more training messages","The offset b alone, so the call is almost arbitrary","The SVM refuses to predict"],"answer":2,"why":"Unseen words have no column, so every xⱼ is 0 and the score is just b. Its sign is a leftover of the fit and its size is near 0. In the widget \"banana pyjamas\" is called technical at C = 1 (b = +0.046) and billing at C = 0.01 (b ≈ −0.0004)."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain how a linear SVM decides whether a new message is about billing or technical problems, and why a word it has never seen does not matter.","model":"The SVM learned one weight per word from the training messages, so a new message gets a score equal to the sum of weight × TF-IDF over its words plus an offset, and the sign gives billing or technical. A word that was not in the training vocabulary has no weight, so it adds nothing to the sum."},"cheat":"Text becomes a TF-IDF vector (count × idf, then length 1). A linear SVM learns one weight per word: score = Σ w·tfidf + b, sign = class, |score| ≥ 1 outside the street. Unseen words add 0. With more words than messages, training accuracy is 100% and every message is a support vector, so judge on held-out data. Fit the vectoriser on training text only."}});
